\par\par \b \uc2 Firms Open Offices for Remote Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry \par \uc2 821 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Caroline Giese decamped to Durham, N.C., from Seattle during the first wave of Covid-19 in 2020 to live and work remotely. Roughly two years later, her office followed her there.\par \par \uc2 Rather than requiring Giese to return to the West Coast office, employer \uc2 Boston Consulting Group\uc2  in October 2022 rented dedicated \uc2 WeWork\uc2  office space for Raleigh-Durham area workers, most of whom relocated there during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 BCG\uc2 , a global company with 29 offices in the U.S., also has opened new offices in Nashville, Tenn., San Diego and Brooklyn, N.Y., in the past two years. The four locations -- two of which are at co-working facilities -- provide office space for more than 300 workers close to where they want to live, the firm said.\par \par \uc2 "My commute is about 10 minutes. It cuts down travel time. There's more time for pre- and post-work activities," Giese said. "It just feels like a more tightknit, sustainable place to live."\par \par \uc2 Giese, a 34-year-old management consultant with clients in the Southeast, isn't required to be at the office a minimum number of days each week but likes having the option when she isn't traveling. "Being 100% remote was really hard because a lot of our job requires collaboration and brainstorming and working together," said Giese. "Learning and development is so much more accelerated when we can be in person."\par \par \uc2 Companies and workers are still trying to find the right mix of in-person, hybrid and remote work more than three years after the pandemic hit. With the unemployment rate hovering near a half-century low, the tight labor market means employers are in a delicate dance with workers over the optimal amount of work-from-home flexibility.\par \par \uc2 The number of available job openings exceeded unemployed job seekers by 3.3 million in August, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 Studies show that workers value the freedom and flexibility of remote work, in some cases more than a pay raise. And they have held on to that flexibility: Offices in 10 major U.S. cities were occupied on average at around 50% in late September, according to weekly data from security-provider \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Employers, meanwhile, are pushing to get workers into the office more often, with many stepping up surveillance of office attendance. Businesses fear that productivity and company culture suffer when workers are dispersed.\par \par \uc2 Companies such as Zoom, \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  owned \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  -- all early pioneers of remote work -- are ordering more employees back to the office. \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  this summer told workers that they might have to relocate to main offices concentrated in bigger cities.\par \par \uc2 "None of us have figured out exactly the right combination of hybrid work," said Brian Gross, \uc2 BCG\uc2 's chief operating officer for North America. "Where we landed is that some amount of time together is really important. People found flexibility [during the pandemic] and want to keep that."\par \par \uc2 An August survey conducted by BCG Henderson Institute, the consulting firm's think tank, found that office workers from different companies who were dissatisfied with their work flexibility were more than twice as likely to consider leaving their organization in the next year compared with those who were satisfied.\par \par \uc2 Although employees in those satellite offices often don't work on the same teams, staff members are exposed to company culture and have opportunities to collaborate, Gross said. \uc2 BCG\uc2 's new offices haven't meant downsizing other locations, he said.\par \par \uc2 Companies can save costs by renting co-working space or adding offices in cities with lower rent, said Travis Howell, an \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2  assistant professor who studies co-working trends. "I think we're going to start seeing more companies rent desks at co-working spaces for their employees or give their employees a 'co-working stipend' where they can choose their own space," he said.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , which requires most workers globally to be in the office three days a week, considered opening smaller locations in New York's Nassau and Westchester counties, a short distance from the Manhattan headquarters, but the bank ultimately abandoned the idea.\par \par \uc2 "We explored it, but our hope and desire was always to bring people back when it was safe," said \uc2 Edward Skyler\uc2 , \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's head of enterprise services and public affairs. "Banking is an apprenticeship business. People really learn from being around each other."\par \par \uc2 Umbra, a California-based satellite maker and data seller, has a co-working space outside Washington, D.C., "for traffic so people don't have to deal with it," said Gabe Dominocielo, the company's founder and president. The company also has a government-affairs office in Washington, which it is expanding.\par \par \uc2 He said the cost of setting up new workspaces to accommodate workers' living preferences and shorten commutes was "so marginal relative to having happy people -- it's just nothing."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw539\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8085\pichgoal7200
