\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Healthcare Workers to Return to Work\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melanie Evans \par \uc2 550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nurses, pharmacists and other \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  employees have followed auto workers, Hollywood writers and other union members going on strike. But they are taking a decidedly different tack on when to end their walkout.\par \par \uc2 The healthcare strikers plan to be back to work on Saturday -- whether they win new contracts or not.\par \par \uc2 More than 75,000 Kaiser employees stopped work on Wednesday, the largest healthcare strike on record. Those in Virginia and Washington, D.C., have already returned to work, while the rest are staying off the job through Friday.\par \par \uc2 The plans might seem to undercut the healthcare unions' pursuit of higher wages and more staffing -- large strikes in manufacturing, construction and retail typically run longer than three days -- but union members and labor experts say there is a larger strategy at work: keeping patients safe and the public on their side.\par \par \uc2 "They don't want to leave their patients high and dry," said Janice Fine, a \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2  labor studies and employment relations professor.\par \par \uc2 The strategy is calculated to squeeze employers and avoid hurting patient care and angering patients whose appointments get canceled, labor experts say. Healthcare unions are sensitive to the burden walkouts put on patients and risk losing that public support if a strike wears on.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  unions and members have said their work stoppage is as much about serving patients -- by seeking more hiring that will reduce wait times and improve the quality of care -- as about increasing the workers' wages.\par \par \uc2 "Us walking off the job and going on this strike is patient advocacy," said Angelica Mateo, a nurse at a Kaiser medical office building in Pasadena, Calif., where she says patients must wait a month or longer for primary care and specialist follow up appointments. "It's us advocating for their safety."\par \par \uc2 Every health system has been facing staffing shortages and fighting burnout, a Kaiser spokeswoman said. She said the system has been hiring new employees and offers leading compensation.\par \par \uc2 The union hopes the three-day strike and other planned labor activity are enough to push negotiations forward, said a union spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 The unions' patient-first message resonated with Jennifer Cole, who says Kaiser rescheduled an eye appointment this week because of the strike.\par \par \uc2 "I want them to get paid, and I want them to be well-staffed so that they can take care of all of us," said Cole, 57, of Northern Virginia. She now is scheduled to visit the Kaiser eye clinic in Fairfax, Va., at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 But just the first days of the strike have been disruptive for patients. Casey Dunn drove to a Kaiser pharmacy in downtown Sacramento Thursday to find it closed, even though Kaiser had notified her the prescription was ready, she says.\par \par \uc2 Dunn says she was on hold with a Kaiser telephone representative for hours to learn what to do, then had to wait with a crowd at a pharmacy to pick up her prescription.\par \par \uc2 She blames Kaiser, saying it knew the strike was coming and could have done more to mitigate the disruption.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser appreciates the patience of members who have been dealing with call center wait times, appointment rescheduling and temporarily closed departments or offices, a representative said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231007eja70004r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
