\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Kaiser's Single-Payer Portent\b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  is often hailed on the political left as a model for single-payer healthcare in the U.S. Like their unionized brethren at \uc2 Britain's National Health Service\uc2  , some 75,000 Kaiser workers this week walked off the job and stranded sick patients.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser is America's largest managed integrated health system, whereby the same entity pays for and provides medical care. Patients with Kaiser insurance are treated by doctors and nurses employed by Kaiser at facilities operated by Kaiser. The consortium serves nearly 13 million patients nationwide, mostly on the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 Its managed-care system has been hailed as less expensive than standard private insurance. Kaiser controls spending in part by restricting patient access to outside providers and typically requiring primary-care physicians to authorize specialty care. The flip side is that sick patients may have to wait longer for treatment.\par \par \uc2 Its single-payer model has other adverse effects. Unions say it skimps on staff, resulting in lower-quality care. It's not unusual for unions to complain about staffing levels since they want employers to hire more workers to boost their membership. But Kaiser's model is intentionally lean.\par \par \uc2 Patients "are telling you how long it took to get the appointment, and then you have to tell them how long it will be to get results," an ultrasound technician who has worked at Kaiser for 27 years told CBS News. "There's a breakdown in the quality of care."\par \par \uc2 U.S. hospitals are increasingly contracting out emergency care to staffing companies to meet patient ebbs and flows. But unions oppose this arrangement because it makes it harder to organize workers. Kaiser's unions also don't seem to like the alternative, which is to employ fewer workers and require them to pick up the slack when demand for care increases.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser's strike is the largest by healthcare workers in U.S. history, and patients will suffer the most harm. "Non-urgent" operations and chemotherapy treatments this week were rescheduled, in some cases reportedly for months later. But because patients are locked into the system, the stakes are lower for both the union and company.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser has offered to increase wages by between 12.5% and 16% over four years, but the unions are demanding a 24.5% raise. Medicare payment cuts and the end of the national pandemic emergency will likely crimp its revenue. Other hospitals and physicians can bill private insurance more to offset government payment reductions, but Kaiser can't.\par \par \uc2 Single-payer healthcare only works until the reality of rationing bites.\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: Healthcare, From Rural Alabama to the Bay" -- WSJ October 10, 2023)\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Why Do I Pay So Much for Kaiser Healthcare?" -- WSJ Oct. 11, 2023)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231006eja600032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
