\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 GM\uc2  Gets $6 Billion Revolving Credit Line\b0\par\par\uc2 By Will Feuer \par \uc2 221 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  entered into a new, $6 billion revolving credit agreement as the automaker grapples with the fallout from the continuing \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike.\par \par \uc2 The automaker said the 364-day revolving credit agreement includes certain restrictions on potential mergers or sales of assets and debt incurrence. Under the agreement, \uc2 GM\uc2  also must maintain global liquidity of at least $4 billion and U.S. liquidity of more than $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 GM\uc2  spokesman said the strike didn't affect third-quarter sales, but it cost the company about $200 million during the quarter. The company is scheduled to post quarterly results on Oct. 24.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  and its peers \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  are reeling from the \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike. About 25,000 workers are on strike at facilities owned by the three companies.\par \par \uc2 The union initiated the work stoppages after it failed to reach terms on new four-year contracts by the Sept. 14 deadline. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has gradually expanded the strike as a pressure tactic.\par \par \uc2 In response, Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2  laid off workers. On Tuesday, \uc2 GM\uc2  said it would idle 163 workers in Ohio due to the strike.\par \par \uc2 Across suppliers and the automakers, over 6,000 factory workers are off the job due to the spillover effects of the strikes.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231005eja50006s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
