\par\par \b \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  Workers Launch Largest Health Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melanie Evans \par \uc2 807 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 75,000 nurses, pharmacists and other employees of the \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  health system walked off the job Wednesday in the largest U.S. healthcare strike on record.\par \par \uc2 The workers struck after contracts expired and their unions couldn't reach an agreement with Kaiser on how much a new deal would increase wages and staffing.\par \par \uc2 To minimize the impact on patients, Kaiser said it has brought on thousands of temporary workers to fill some vacancies, but would, if needed, postpone some appointments and expand its network to retail pharmacies and, for some people, non-Kaiser hospitals.\par \par \uc2 "Our plans ensure that the urgent needs of our members and patients are the top priority," Kaiser said.\par \par \uc2 After the strike started, the system notified patients where to expect closures, including pharmacies, ambulatory surgery centers and other services across more than two dozen locations.\par \par \uc2 Roxanne Hawn and her husband canceled his appointment Wednesday at a Kaiser clinic south of Denver to avoid crossing the picket line.\par \par \uc2 "I come from a union family," Hawn said. "It's an insult to the people picketing." Her husband must now wait until early November, said Hawn, a freelance writer.\par \par \uc2 Edith Hurtado, an obstetric-gynecology medical assistant for Kaiser, joined the picket line in front of Kaiser Permanente San Francisco Medical Center with the goal of addressing staff shortages. "We are all here to send Kaiser a message," Hurtado said.\par \par \uc2 Acting U.S. Secretary of Labor Julie Su has met with both sides to help resolve the strike, and is seeking to move the talks forward to reach a resolution, the department said. The unions and Kaiser confirmed her involvement.\par \par \uc2 The strike, which is scheduled to last as long as three days, adds hospitals, pharmacies and clinics to workplaces roiled by labor action this year, after auto workers and Hollywood writers stopped work.\par \par \uc2 Through August this year, the U.S. had lost more workdays to labor disputes than any full year since 2000.\par \par \uc2 Unions emboldened by public support and the tight labor market are flexing their power to demand higher pay. Workers have also seen how strikes by other unions have secured contract wins.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser, based in Oakland, Calif., is a well-known name in healthcare, pioneering the combination of a health insurer, hospitals and doctor's offices under one roof in a bid to offer higher-quality care while controlling costs.\par \par \uc2 The system serves 12.7 million members at 40 hospitals and more than 620 medical offices, mostly on the West Coast but also in Colorado, Georgia, Hawaii, Maryland and Virginia. Kaiser counts about 213,000 employees who aren't physicians.\par \par \uc2 The work stoppage involves Kaiser workers in five states and Washington, D.C., including workers who care for patients, such as pharmacists and respiratory therapists, and other staffers, such as laboratory technicians and kitchen and janitorial employees.\par \par \uc2 Workers in Washington, D.C., and Virginia plan to strike for a day, a spokeswoman for the unions said. Strikes in California, Colorado, Oregon and Washington state are expected to last three days.\par \par \uc2 The strike is the largest action by healthcare workers since 1993, when the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  first collected such data.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser unions said inflation has eroded employee wages, and staff shortages are burning out workers while compromising the quality of care.\par \par \uc2 Georgette Bradford, an ultrasound technologist at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  in Sacramento, Calif., who is a member of the unions' bargaining team, said short staffing in her unit has prompted some patients to leave without getting care.\par \par \uc2 Bradford said workers are demanding that Kaiser do more to boost hiring and keep workers, such as offering better raises.\par \par \uc2 "The cost of living is not matching the current wages," she said. "We're losing people."\par \par \uc2 Kaiser said its compensation leads its markets and the company has increased hiring. It has hired 10,000 people this year for jobs covered by unions, a spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 "We are committed to addressing every area of staffing that is still challenging," she said.\par \par \uc2 Like other health systems, Kaiser has faced a shortage of healthcare workers coming out of the Covid-19 pandemic but has added more than 50,000 employees to its payroll in the past two years and continues to hire, the spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 In the contract talks, Kaiser said, it has proposed wage increases of 12.5% to 16% in total over four years, depending on the location.\par \par \uc2 Unions said the employer's offer doesn't go far enough to offset inflation.\par \par \uc2 They are seeking a total wage increase of 24.5%.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser's finances this year rebounded from an industrywide drop seen in 2022 from the tight labor market and inflation.\par \par \uc2 Kaiser reported net income of $3.3 billion on $50.4 billion in revenue during the first half of this year.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Austen Hufford contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich325\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4875
