\par\par \b \uc2 Criminals Infiltrate Workforce At Airports\b0\par\par\uc2 By Benjamin Katz \par \uc2 926 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a restroom stall in the departure hall of St. Thomas airport in the U.S. Virgin Islands, a recently hired worker waited in January with two taped-up bricks of cocaine until he heard the signal: stomping and knocking.\par \par \uc2 The airport worker reached down and passed the packages, weighing a total of nearly 5 pounds, under the partition separating him from the adjoining stall. His accomplice, whose face he wasn't meant to see, stuffed them into a red backpack to take with him on a \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  flight to Orlando, Fla.\par \par \uc2 Both were apprehended before the accomplice could board his flight. Court documents show that the worker, who had been with Hyannis, Mass.-based \uc2 Cape Air\uc2  for less than a year, was set to make $2,000 for getting the drugs into the departure hall.\par \par \uc2 The incident is among a spate of criminal activity since the recovery in air travel that has involved so-called airport insiders, those who have access to areas that for passengers would require security checks or be off-limits.\par \par \uc2 As air travel rebounds from its pandemic hibernation, the aviation industry has grappled with widespread disruption, near collisions of aircraft and rising workplace injuries. It is also wrestling with the growing threat of criminal gangs recruiting airport workers, according to law-enforcement authorities, government agencies and security specialists.\par \par \uc2 "This is a huge challenge," said Peter Nilsson, head of Airpol, a European law-enforcement group tasked with fighting crime at airports. Tackling insider threats stemming from rapid hiring at airports is among Airpol's postpandemic priorities, he said.\par \par \uc2 "We raised a warning when, after the pandemic, we had all airports more or less in the world rehiring people," he said.\par \par \uc2 Similar warnings about criminal groups targeting airport workers to be their accessories have come from the \uc2 U.S. Transportation Security Administration\uc2 , the U.K.'s National Crime Agency and Airports Council International, an industry group.\par \par \uc2 Airport workers are particularly attractive recruits for criminal organizations. Experts point to examples where baggage handlers have been recruited to shift contraband-filled suitcases from international flights onto domestic carousels, bypassing checks by customs security; runway workers have been tasked with planting narcotics in the panels of aircraft; and customs officials have been paid to wave through bags known to contain illicit items.\par \par \uc2 While there has always been some degree of threat posed by insiders, experts said the mass hiring after the pandemic has given criminal entities the chance to plant or recruit new accomplices.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of thousands of new staff have joined the aviation industry, typically with less experience and security know-how. There has also been an increase in the use of temporary security passes, requiring less-thorough screening, to ease staffing shortages.\par \par \uc2 "You have the same criminal activities or the same terrorist groups that already wanted to attack aviation. They will find new people," said Sonia Hifdi, head of aviation security policy at the \uc2 International Civil Aviation Organization\uc2 , the United Nations' aviation arm.\par \par \uc2 The combination of low pay at a time of inflation and dissatisfaction among airport workers amid the postpandemic recovery is also making some more susceptible to criminal recruiters, experts warned.\par \par \uc2 "You've got unhappy people, people desperate for money and criminal organizations looking to expand their reach," said Andy Blackwell, an analyst at ISARR, an aviation security specialist. "It's fertile ground right now."\par \par \uc2 Blackwell said the number of serious security incidents recorded by ISARR involving airport insiders worldwide so far this year is 48, up from 35 for all of 2022. Those include a cargo handler in Miami being arrested after allegedly stealing $1.2 million worth of \uc2 Samsung\uc2  cellphones from a \uc2 DHL\uc2  freight container, and two \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  bag handlers in San Francisco being charged with removing marijuana from checked luggage and sneaking it out disguised as trash in 20-gallon bin bags.\par \par \uc2 Security training was difficult during the pandemic, and there has been pressure on resources to provide that training to new staff since then, which the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .'s \uc2 ICAO\uc2  said has added to the vulnerabilities. Workers who left their jobs while on furlough but who still have access codes to secure areas of airports and insider knowledge of operations also pose a risk, the agency said.\par \par \uc2 "If you ask an airport security manager what keeps them awake at night, most would say the insider threat," said Philip Baum, managing director of aviation-security consulting firm Green Light.\par \par \uc2 In Belgium, two baggage handlers were arrested in July after being caught offloading suitcases stacked with 265 pounds of cocaine. In Portugal, runway workers in Lisbon were arrested in December for allegedly operating a similar scheme after being noted by authorities for their lavish spending. In Canada, about $15 million in gold and other high-value goods were stolen in April from an \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  restricted-access cargo facility at Toronto Pearson Airport.\par \par \uc2 Not all crime committed by insiders is orchestrated by external groups. In July, three \uc2 TSA\uc2  security officers at \uc2 Miami International Airport\uc2  were caught on CCTV palming items from hand-luggage bins into their pockets. Footage shows the workers allegedly stealing $600 in cash and other high-value goods from passengers' bags as they adjusted items in trays before sending them through X-ray machines.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 TSA\uc2  said it had removed the officers and shared findings with police.\par \par \uc2 A new \uc2 TSA\uc2  mandate will require physical screening of workers at most airports, similar to the checks undergone by passengers, which brings the U.S. closer to meeting international standards.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231004eja400038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
