\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Automakers Notch Big Sales Gains --- Car market has yet to see an impact from \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike-related shutdowns\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean McLain \par \uc2 927 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Randy Parker is head of Hyundai Motor's U.S. business. A Page One article on Monday about electric-vehicle sales incorrectly said he is head of the North American business. An Oct. 4 Business News article about auto sales contained the same error.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ Oct. 17, 2023)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 U.S. auto-industry sales continued to rise in the third quarter, with automakers and buyers largely unfazed by higher interest rates and the impact of a continuing \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike.\par \par \uc2 Automakers sold an estimated 3.9 million new cars and trucks during the quarter, up about 17% over the year-earlier period, according to \uc2 J.D. Power\uc2 , a data and analytics firm.\par \par \uc2 American car buyers have continued to flock to dealership lots, despite high car prices and a cooling labor market. The sales strength in the car business reflects a resilience in consumer demand with many people giving priority to short-term needs over longer-term goals.\par \par \uc2 The latest results build on the industry's momentum in the first half of the year, with new-vehicle sales climbing 13% in the January-to-June period after hitting their lowest point in more than a decade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike, which began in mid-September and has targeted five assembly plants at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , has yet to hurt U.S. auto sales significantly.\par \par \uc2 The three Detroit automakers built up inventory ahead of the work stoppage, which so far has affected plants accounting for about 16% of the companies' North American production, according to research firm Wards Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Dealers say they have sufficient supplies of models affected by the factory walkouts, but that could change if the strike goes on and expands further.\par \par \uc2 The picketing is already having ripple effects. On Tuesday, \uc2 GM\uc2  said it would idle 163 additional workers in Ohio due to the continuing \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike, which affects about 25,000 union employees.\par \par \uc2 The automaker said the move affects work at the company's Toledo Propulsion Systems facility, which manufactures transmissions and drive units for assembly plants in Michigan and Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Rhett Ricart, chief executive of \uc2 Ricart Automotive\uc2 , which owns dealerships in Ohio, said he had enough Ford Bronco sport-utility vehicles to last for about two months. If the strike goes longer than 45 days, Ricart said he would be buying used Bronco SUVs to stock his lot.\par \par \uc2 "Broncos are like a \uc2 Jeep\uc2 . I have to have them," he said.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  on Tuesday reported a 21% increase in U.S. sales, a jump largely fueled by its Chevrolet and Buick brands as well as rebounding supplies of its top-selling pickup trucks.\par \par \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2 's U.S. sales climbed 12% in the just-ended quarter, while \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2 's sales rose 53% from a year earlier off strong demand for its smaller, more affordable cars and SUVs.\par \par \uc2 Ford is expected to report its sales on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's U.S. division, which owns \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , Ram, Chrysler and other brands, was the one outlier, reporting a 1% drop in sales in the June-to-September period, led by a decline in demand for its pickup trucks and SUVs.\par \par \uc2 The car company has wrestled with falling sales and market share for much of the year. While the union is on strike at a Jeep plant in Toledo, Ohio, the sales drop in the third quarter was across multiple brands and models.\par \par \uc2 Dealers and car companies say this year's strong sales are partly driven by buyers who put off purchases during a period of vehicle shortages in past years, which pushed many cars to be sold above manufacturer-suggested retail prices.\par \par \uc2 The third-quarter sales results were buoyed by fuller dealer lots, which in turn meant fewer price markups and more discounts on the new-car lot.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the average price paid by car buyers in the third quarter fell slightly from the year-earlier period to $45,516 a vehicle, \uc2 J.D. Power\uc2 's data shows.\par \par \uc2 "There's been a lot of pent-up demand in the marketplace," said Randy Parker, head of \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2 's North American division. "The only things keeping things in check are interest rates, because they're making it harder for customers to afford a vehicle."\par \par \uc2 Several automakers said renewed interest in smaller, lower-priced models, such as subcompact cars and compact SUVs, helped lift sales in the quarter. Executives also said more buyers are opting for eco-friendly alternatives, including fully electric models or hybrids and hybrid plug-ins.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say a rebound in fleet sales also is helping boost results. Sales to commercial fleet operators and rental-car companies slowed last year as car companies directed their limited production toward individual buyers. But now, the fleet side of the business is coming back and growing faster than retail sales, according to research firm \uc2 Cox Automotive\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While overall auto sales have remained strong, the electric-vehicle market is showing signs of cooling off, with sales of battery-powered cars having plateaued at roughly 8.5% of the market in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Some car companies, such as Ford, have seen supplies of their EVs build up. Ford dealers had about 20,500 Mustang Mach-E SUVs in transit or on their lots in August, which equates to about 110 days of unsold inventory, according to Motor Intelligence data. The industry average for all new vehicles was 38 days.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231004eja40006f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
