\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: UAW Strike Hurts Steel Demand --- Drop in purchases by Detroit plants adds pressure to tumbling prices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 638 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The strike against the Detroit automakers is dealing a blow to the U.S. steel market.\par \par \uc2 Steel purchases by automotive-related users began slowing during the summer as a strike by the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  appeared more likely, adding pressure to steel prices that had already been pressured by weakness in manufacturing and commercial construction sectors. The spot-market price for benchmark coiled sheet steel has fallen 40% since April.\par \par \uc2 "The air just got sucked out of the steel market," said \uc2 Jeremy Flack\uc2 , chief executive of Flack Global Metals, a Chicago-based steel distributor. "There's fear in the market and nobody wants to buy anything."\par \par \uc2 Demand for steel from the auto industry had been a bright spot for the steel industry in the past year. Vehicle-production volumes rebounded from supply-chain bottlenecks during the Covid-19 pandemic. Steelmakers have been investing in new and existing mills in anticipation of rising demand for steel from federal infrastructure projects, electric-vehicle battery plants and increased auto production.\par \par \uc2 Steelmakers Nucor and \uc2 Steel Dynamics\uc2  in September forecast third-quarter profit would decline from the same period last year and from the second quarter because of lower steel prices. \uc2 United States Steel\uc2  in September said it would idle a blast furnace at its Granite City, Ill., mill and reallocate the steel produced there to other mills where steel production has dipped because of the auto workers' strike.\par \par \uc2 The spot market price for coiled sheet steel slipped about 3% since the strike started Sept. 15 to $700 a ton, according to S&P Global Commodity Insights.\par \par \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2  on Sept. 27 tried to jolt the market out of its doldrums by announcing it would charge a minimum of $750 a ton for all varieties of its steel. The Cleveland-based steelmaker is typically the industry's most aggressive at raising prices. Other steel companies often follow with their own increases.\par \par \uc2 Industry analysts saw \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2 's move as an attempt to halt further price erosion by betting that steel customers are ready to replenish depleted inventories -- and will pay more.\par \par \uc2 Using price increases to cure a slumping market can be risky if customers simply refuse to pay more or refrain from buying steel. The spot-market steel price last November sank to a low of $620 a ton before rallying into early 2023, according to S&P.\par \par \uc2 The slump has taken a toll on steel company stocks by driving down stocks. \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2  made an unsolicited offer of more than $7 billion for \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  in July. The Pittsburgh-based steelmaker's stock had fallen 27% in the months before \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2  went public in August with a cash-and-stock offer that it said provided \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  investors with a significant premium for their shares.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  has said it is considering several offers of all or parts of the company.\par \par \uc2 A walkout by hourly workers at selected plants operated by \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which includes Chrysler and \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , began after negotiations failed to produce a new contract.\par \par \uc2 Several weeks' worth of vehicle inventory on dealers' lots or in transit when production stopped is expected to leave enough vehicles on hand to sell in the short term.\par \par \uc2 S&P estimates the strike so far has knocked out production of 6,030 vehicles a day that consumed about 5,982 tons of steel.\par \par \uc2 Chris Zuzick, vice president of Waukesha Metal Products, a Wisconsin supplier of brackets for vehicle suspensions, said his company has throttled back its longer-term purchases of steel since the strike. "Right now we're really watching our inventories and not making a lot of commitments for raw materials," he said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich511\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7665
