\par\par \b \uc2 Bankruptcy Tests Dockworkers Union\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 597 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The West Coast dockworkers union's bankruptcy filing puts a dent in the armor of one of the country's most powerful labor groups, an organization that includes 22,000 members who handle virtually all U.S. seaborne trade from Seattle to San Diego.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Longshore and Warehouse Union\uc2  filed for bankruptcy over the weekend to fend off a judgment holding it liable for what a federal jury found to be illegal slowdowns and work stoppages at Oregon's \uc2 Port of Portland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 ILWU\uc2  spokesman said the union will operate as normal "throughout the restructuring process."\par \par \uc2 The union controls the hiring of dockworkers and their daily dispatch to work at cargo-handling terminals at 29 ports from Washington state to California. Its territory includes the nation's busiest container port complex, at Los Angeles and Long Beach, the main gateway for imports of toys, clothes, electronics, furniture and consumer products from Asia.\par \par \uc2 "They have enormous leverage because the members of the union have typically acted in concert," said Paul Bingham, director of transportation consulting at S&P Global Market Intelligence. "If they [struck], the entire coast would be shut down."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ILWU\uc2  has demonstrated during highly contentious contract negotiations that it doesn't need to strike to show its muscle. Its work actions -- slowing or stopping operations -- have helped the union maintain jobs that include some of the most generous wages and benefits of any union in the country. Average annual earnings for a full-time registered longshore worker on the West Coast last year was $211,000, according to the \uc2 Pacific Maritime Association\uc2 , which represents port employers.\par \par \uc2 The bankruptcy filing shows the union's tactics aren't always without risk. The union was found liable at the Oregon port for hurting revenue at a subsidiary of Philippines-based maritime company \uc2 International Container Terminal Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A federal jury in 2019 found that the \uc2 ILWU\uc2  coordinated work slowdowns and stoppages at the terminal because of a dispute over whether \uc2 ILWU\uc2  workers or members of another union would perform work handling refrigerated shipping containers. ICTSI was initially awarded $93.6 million, a sum that was later reduced to $19 million. ICTSI rejected the reduced amount. The \uc2 ILWU\uc2  filed for bankruptcy ahead of a retrial set for next year saying it couldn't afford legal fees to fight the case or any damages that may be awarded.\par \par \uc2 The union's only material asset is its $9.5 million in cash on hand, ILWU President \uc2 Willie Adams\uc2  said in a bankruptcy-court filing in San Francisco. The \uc2 ILWU\uc2  plans to pay most of that cash to ICTSI to resolve their dispute, while retaining a working-capital reserve big enough for the union "to maintain its operations and rebuild," Adams said.\par \par \uc2 Eric Snyder, chair of the bankruptcy department at law firm Wilk Auslander, said the bankruptcy filing could lead the union to think twice before embarking on future work slowdowns. "They must realize now that they are really going to run a risk if they do this again, and that they're going to get caught again in a potential lawsuit that might bankrupt them," Snyder said.\par \par \uc2 Still, the Portland case so far hasn't stopped the union from continuing its tactic of slowing and stopping operations in disputes with employers, according to port employers.\par \par \uc2 During drawn-out negotiations over a new multiyear contract for West Coast dockworkers that began in 2022 and ended this summer, terminal operators accused \uc2 ILWU\uc2  members of deliberately withholding labor or slowing operations.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Andrew Scurria contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231003eja30000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
