\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Dockworkers Union Files for Bankruptcy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Scurria \par \uc2 462 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The labor union for West Coast dockworkers filed for bankruptcy to fend off a judgment holding it liable for what a federal jury found to be illegal slowdowns and work stoppages at the \uc2 Port of Portland\uc2  in Oregon.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Longshore and Warehouse Union\uc2  filed for chapter 11 protection in a San Francisco court to halt litigation brought by a former terminal operator of the \uc2 Port of Portland\uc2 , Ore., that threatened to deplete the labor union's cash reserves.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ILWU\uc2 , which represents dockworkers at major U.S. ports in Los Angeles, Long Beach and Oakland, Calif., is fresh off ratifying a new, six-year contractfor about 22,000 workers at 29 ports from California to Washington state to end a period of labor uncertainty for some of the nation's busiest ports.\par \par \uc2 The dockworkers union also has been facing a looming trial on claims that it illegally slowed down operations over several years at the \uc2 Port of Portland\uc2 , then operated by an affiliate of Philippines-based maritime company \uc2 International Container Terminal Services Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bankruptcy filing puts ICTSI's lawsuit against the union on hold and opens a path for it to limit its liability and reorganize its affairs. The \uc2 ILWU\uc2  said it filed for chapter 11 because it couldn't afford the legal fees to continue fighting the case and because any damages award would likely exceed what it can pay.\par \par \uc2 The union was held liable in 2019 to ICTSI for engaging in unfair labor practices aimed at securing work handling refrigerated shipping containers, known as reefer work.\par \par \uc2 ICTSI's lawsuit dates to 2012 and accused the \uc2 ILWU\uc2  and its local chapter of engaging in work stoppages, slowdowns and other actions to try to pressure the \uc2 Port of Portland\uc2  to have reefer work done by the union.\par \par \uc2 The company was initially awarded $93.6 million, later reduced to $19 million in a court ruling that offered the company the opportunity for a retrial solely on the issue of damages. ICTSI turned down the reduced award and opted to continue litigating its claims that the union intentionally drove ocean-carrier customers away from Portland. A retrial was scheduled for February 2024.\par \par \uc2 ILWU President \uc2 Willie Adams\uc2  said that while it has attempted to resolve the decadeold lawsuit, "at this point the union can no longer afford to defend against ICTSI's scorched-earth litigation tactic."\par \par \uc2 ICTSI Oregon, the company unit that sued the \uc2 ILWU\uc2 , said on Sunday that the union "refused to appreciate the gravity of the harm that it intentionally caused and make settlement offers reflective of that harm." "This bankruptcy is the \uc2 ILWU\uc2 's attempt to once again shirk its responsibility," ICTSI Oregon said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Paul Berger contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231002eja20000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
