\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- \uc2 UAW\uc2  Expands Strike at \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert and Ryan Felton \par \uc2 797 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  expanded its strike to a \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  factory in Chicago and a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  plant in Michigan, citing a lack of progress at the bargaining table.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain said during a livestream address Friday that Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  would be spared from further walkouts for now, saying the company delivered a proposal that made significant progress on the union's demands, including cost-of-living adjustments.\par \par \uc2 The new walkouts would add nearly 7,000 workers to the roughly 18,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2  members on strike across the three companies, at three assembly plants and dozens of parts-distribution facilities. The Detroit companies combined have about 146,000 union workers.\par \par \uc2 The union renewed its expansion of strikes at Ford after sparing the automaker last week because Fain said talks had been advancing at the time.\par \par \uc2 The latest walkouts offer further insight into the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's novel strike strategy. The union has been gradually expanding the walkouts but so far without causing deep disruption across the companies' factory footprints.\par \par \uc2 The union also has applied pressure unevenly, rewarding companies that give up more ground in talks.\par \par \uc2 The latest expansion drew sharp rebukes from the chief executives of \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford Friday, with both accusing the union of prioritizing publicity over reaching a deal for members.\par \par \uc2 The latest work stoppages hit plants that make popular seven-passenger SUV models. Ford's Chicago factory builds the Explorer and Lincoln Aviator. \uc2 GM\uc2 's factory in Lansing, Mich., makes the Chevrolet Traverse and Buick Enclave.\par \par \uc2 The union stopped short of targeting factories that produce the companies' big pickup trucks and largest SUVs. Those vehicles, including Ford's F-series trucks and \uc2 GM\uc2 's Chevy Suburban SUVs, are the companies' biggest moneymakers.\par \par \uc2 "We will win. Our strategy is working," Fain told tens of thousands of viewers in his livestream address Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 Tensions between Ford and the union flared this past week after the company said it was pausing construction at a battery plant in Michigan, citing concerns about its ability to remain competitive. Fain criticized the move as a threat to cut jobs.\par \par \uc2 Ford Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  expressed frustration Friday about the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's latest action, saying he believed the two sides could have reached a compromise on pay and benefits. Instead, he said talks hit a sticking point when they turned to future jobs at the company's planned battery plants.\par \par \uc2 "So far, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is holding the deal hostage over battery plants," Farley said in a news conference Friday afternoon. He added that the car company wanted to determine first what its labor costs would look like in the next union agreement before moving ahead with the Michigan battery plant.\par \par \uc2 Farley also took a swipe at Fain, saying he is more interested in media attention than getting a deal done. "Shawn has been on the TV more than Jake from State Farm," Farley said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  president fired back in a statement, saying the CEO is lying about the state of negotiations and had failed to show up at bargaining this week.\par \par \uc2 "If he were there, he'd know we gave Ford a comprehensive proposal on Monday and still haven't heard back," Fain said. He added, alluding to the State Farm jingle: "Like a good neighbor, we're available 24/7."\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2 's Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  released a pointed statement, accusing the union of dragging out the strikes for "their own personal and political agendas."\par \par \uc2 "It's clear that there is no real intent to get to an agreement," Barra said.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  said it hasn't received a full counteroffer from the union since the company submitted its last proposal on Sept. 21.\par \par \uc2 Fain said \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's proposal came closer to the union's demands on cost-of-living adjustments, the right to strike over plant closures and job outsourcing. The new union president said he is excited about the momentum at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , whose earlier proposals he had strongly rebuked.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which absorbed \uc2 Fiat Chrysler\uc2  in 2021, sells \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , Ram, Chrysler and Dodge brands in the U.S. The company is also a major player in Europe and other overseas markets, where it sells \uc2 Peugeot\uc2 , Citroen, \uc2 Opel\uc2  and other names.\par \par \uc2 The company is more global than \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford, deriving roughly half of its operating profit from outside North America. It also has the fewest hourly \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers of the three companies.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has sought to bring national attention to its walkouts while preserving its $825 million fund used to compensate striking workers and withholding the most serious harm to the companies' bottom lines, said Marc Robinson, a consultant who spent more than 30 years at \uc2 GM\uc2 , including on labor strategy.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich296\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4440
