\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Las Vegas Workers Vote to Strike --- No walk-off date has been set as contract talks with casinos continue\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dean Seal \par \uc2 330 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Las Vegas Strip workers voted to authorize a possible strike as union contract negotiations continue with casino companies including \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2  and \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Culinary and Bartenders unions said late Tuesday that 95% of members voted to give union leadership the authority to call a strike for about 53,000 Las Vegas housekeepers, bartenders and other workers if a deal isn't reached.\par \par \uc2 The Culinary Union hasn't yet set a strike deadline and said it continues to negotiate in good faith with the gambling companies. There is no guarantee that a strike will occur.\par \par \uc2 More talks are scheduled for next week, Culinary Union Secretary-Treasurer Ted Pappageorge said.\par \par \uc2 "If these gambling companies don't come to an agreement, the workers have spoken and we will be ready to do whatever it takes -- up to and including a strike," Pappageorge said.\par \par \uc2 Workers are seeking new, five-year contracts that include "the largest wage increases ever negotiated in the history of the Culinary Union," according to a statement from the group. The Culinary and Bartenders unions negotiate together.\par \par \uc2 They are also bargaining for reductions in housekeeping workloads, more on-site security to protect workers from criminal behavior by customers and extended rights to return to their jobs in the event of another pandemic or economic crisis, the Culinary Union said.\par \par \uc2 About 40,000 of the 53,000 workers are employed by the big three casino companies on the Strip -- \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2 , Caesars and \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2  -- and their contracts expired over the summer.\par \par \uc2 The casino and hotel operators didn't return a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The last time Las Vegas Strip workers walked off the job was in 1984, with 17,000 Culinary Union members on strike for 67 days. The Culinary and Bartender unions represent 60,000 workers in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Katherine Sayre contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230928ej9s0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
