\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Biden Joins Auto Union Picket Line --- Move is a first for a U.S. president, as he and Trump court a crucial voter group\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey and Ken Thomas \par \uc2 873 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BELLEVILLE, Mich. -- President Biden walked a Michigan picket line with striking auto workers on Tuesday, offering an unprecedented display of union support as he and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  prepared for a fight for working-class voters.\par \par \uc2 Biden joined members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  at a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  customer-care center, a politically risky step that makes him the first U.S. president to walk a picket line, according to the White House and historians.\par \par \uc2 "Stick with it. You deserve the significant raise you need and other benefits," Biden said, addressing members of UAW Local 174 through a bullhorn. Biden, wearing a black \uc2 UAW\uc2  baseball cap, then put his arm around a worker wearing a red \uc2 UAW\uc2  T-shirt and listened as union President Shawn Fain called Biden's visit "a historic moment."\par \par \uc2 "Our president has chosen to stand up with workers in our fight for economic and social justice," Fain said, thanking Biden for his attendance. Union members waved signs with messages including "Saving The American Dream," cheered for the president and lined up to fist bump him after the remarks.\par \par \uc2 Asked by a reporter whether workers deserved a 40% pay raise, Biden said, "Yes," adding: "I think they should be able to bargain for that."\par \par \uc2 More than a year before the general election, Biden and Trump are making plays for the battleground state, which Trump captured in 2016 and Biden won in 2020. Trump is dominating the Republican primary field and, after Biden's pitch to auto workers in the Detroit area, the former president was expected in the state on Wednesday as hypothetical general election polls show them running neck and neck.\par \par \uc2 For Biden, who has declared himself the most pro-union president in history, the trip comes as he is struggling in the polls, with voters expressing doubts about his age and handling of the economy. His decision to walk the picket line carried risks if the strike drags on.\par \par \uc2 Trump, who holds a commanding lead in the Republican primaries despite efforts by some in the party to rally around an alternative, is to address current and former auto workers Wednesday in Clinton Township, a community in Macomb County northeast of Detroit. The event will serve as counterprogramming to the second Republican primary debate in California, and comes as he pushes to siphon off union support from Biden.\par \par \uc2 In a written statement on Tuesday, Trump blamed workers' problems on the push by Biden and Democrats to promote electric vehicles. "Crooked Joe should be ashamed to show his face before these hardworking Americans he is stabbing in the back," he said.\par \par \uc2 Biden and Trump have begun focusing more intensely on each other as the election shapes up as a 2020 redux. Biden has begun targeting Trump by name over issues such as abortion and going after his White House record, as he signals a more aggressive approach toward his expected opponent. Trump's team is welcoming the direct conflict, which they say elevates him in the race, though they are still competing in early states such as Iowa as they seek to lock up the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 Trump announced his Michigan visit first, and his campaign claimed Biden was following his lead; Biden's team said choosing to visit the picket line was something the president wanted to do and wasn't driven by Trump's plans to visit the state.\par \par \uc2 "President Biden was invited by the head of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to come. He is listening to workers, he is leaning in with workers," said Rep. Haley Stevens (D., Mich.).\par \par \uc2 While the candidates are focused on Michigan this week, the effort to win union workers is also a factor in other crucial states, such as Wisconsin and Pennsylvania, where labor often plays a pivotal role in contests.\par \par \uc2 The auto strike highlights the difficult line Biden is trying to walk as he seeks to bolster his pro-union bona fides while also presenting himself as a careful steward of the economy.\par \par \uc2 "What Biden is doing is going to Detroit, holding up a sign and answering the slogan . . . 'Which side are you on?' And he's saying, '\uc2 UAW\uc2 ,'" said \uc2 Douglas Brinkley\uc2 , a presidential historian. "It's a windfall for him if this strike gets settled in the next month. If the strike drags on, and ends up cracking our economy, the photo of Biden on a picket line will be used by the Republicans to say that it's the 'Biden recession.'"\par \par \uc2 The Big Three auto companies have sought to distance themselves from the Biden-Trump visits.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 General Motors\uc2  spokesman said the company's focus is "not on politics" but on "bargaining in good faith with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  leadership to reach an agreement as quickly as possible."\par \par \uc2 Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  pointed to its proposals to \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers and cited the need for "a balanced agreement." Ford said that in response to "political and media attention," it would "stay focused on reaching a deal that is fair to our employees and enables us to invest and grow."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230927ej9r0003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
