\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Five Clues to Where the Strike Is Headed Next --- Detroit's carmakers face an escalating fight with the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  at a critical moment in their history. Here's what to watch for in the labor dispute as the ripples spread.\b0\par\par\uc2 1,788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Ann Marie Uetz is a Detroit-based lawyer at Foley & Lardner. An Exchange article on Saturday incorrectly gave her first name as Ann-Marie, with a hyphen.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ Sept. 26, 2023)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 The auto workers strike is filled with wild cards.\par \par \uc2 A new union boss. Carmakers navigating a costly transition to EVs. Car dealers and auto-parts suppliers caught in the middle. And it is all happening amid a period of drastic change in how cars are made -- and how we buy them.\par \par \uc2 These are some of the key factors driving events as the fight escalates, with the union striking at 38 additional locations on Friday. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has never before conducted walkouts at all three Detroit automakers, Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , at once. \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  President Shawn Fain is driving that strategy, and has broken with the Detroit union's traditions in other ways as well.\par \par \uc2 The union is seeking a 40% pay increase over four years, while the companies are offering 20%. Fain also wants the re-establishment of benefits lost in previous negotiations. Is he asking too much? Detroit's three car companies are outspending nonunionized rivals on labor costs amid an expensive technological shift, and the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's demands risk pushing those costs higher.\par \par \uc2 Auto suppliers and car dealerships have been rebuilding their inventories after the Covid-19 pandemic ravaged the supply chain, but more cutbacks in orders and payments could create financial blows that could topple some companies.\par \par \uc2 To make sense of the unfolding situation, here are five areas to watch out for.\par \par \uc2 1. A FORD DEAL FIRST\par \par \uc2 The company likely to reach a new tentative labor deal with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  first is Ford.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 's decision Friday to spare Ford while calling more strikes at the other two automakers signaled that a tentative agreement could be close, although both sides said significant gaps remain on key issues.\par \par \uc2 Ford and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  have historically had a relatively positive relationship, in part due to some key differences with crosstown rivals \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . For one, Ford employs more hourly unionized workers in the U.S. than its competitors.\par \par \uc2 Ford also builds its brand around supporting its factory workforce. \uc2 William Clay Ford Jr\uc2 ., the executive chair of the company and great-grandson of \uc2 Henry Ford\uc2 , plays ice hockey with \uc2 UAW\uc2  members.\par \par \uc2 The company has also historically dodged strikes -- its last nationwide strike before Fain's recent targeted walkout was in 1976.\par \par \uc2 While it has endured a single-factory strike in this round of talks, the company has been somewhat shielded from Fain's ire.\par \par \uc2 Ford executives are negotiating with \uc2 Chuck Browning\uc2 , who was recently re-elected to the role, and has built a relationship with the company before this round of talks. Other companies are confronting new union bargaining leaders who were elected in the past year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has filed complaints against \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , accusing them of not bargaining in good faith. Ford hasn't faced the same action.\par \par \uc2 -- Nora Eckert\par \par \uc2 2. THE WILD-CARD CARMAKER\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  could confront its biggest challenge at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , a company with a globe-trotting chief executive, \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2 , who has shown he is willing to play hardball in negotiations and has the leverage of a diversified manufacturing business that spans five continents.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , created out of the merger of \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2  and \uc2 PSA Group\uc2  in 2021, is the world's fourth-largest automaker and is less reliant on North America than Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2 , which generate the bulk of their sales in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Last year, it was the most profitable of the three car companies in North America -- a change from Chrysler, which had historically been the smallest and most financially challenged of the Detroit rivals. That gives the union some leverage to argue that it can afford to give back more to the workers.\par \par \uc2 Still, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  also has excess manufacturing capacity in Mexico and Canada, giving it flexibility to consider moving some work to those countries if labor costs in the U.S. get too high.\par \par \uc2 The Portuguese-born Tavares is mostly based in Europe, where he has developed a reputation as a hard-nosed negotiator and adroit cost-cutter. He is used to dealing with Europe's powerful labor unions, and this is \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's first go at bargaining a contract with the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . He also doesn't have strong ties to Michigan, like his peers at \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, he idled a Jeep plant in Illinois, angering the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . He cited the high costs of shifting to electric vehicles, and the factory's fate has become a focal point during talks. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has additionally proposed selling its North American headquarters, where some \uc2 UAW\uc2  members are based, and closing 18 other parts and distribution facilities.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has taken aim at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , accusing it of not bargaining in good faith and holding a mock picket line outside the Auburn Hills, Mich., headquarters on Wednesday. Fain himself also started his union career as an electrician at a Chrysler plant in Kokomo, Ind., where he grew up. That plant was absorbed by \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  in the merger.\par \par \uc2 -- Ryan Felton\par \par \uc2 3. PARTS SUPPLIERS\par \par \uc2 Automotive suppliers could be more vulnerable to a prolonged strike than the carmakers.\par \par \uc2 While rising car prices have fueled record profits at automakers, the same hasn't been true for their parts suppliers. That is because parts makers sign up for long-term supply contracts at a fixed price, which means they have been squeezed by rising prices for raw materials and labor with little ability to pass along those higher costs.\par \par \uc2 "The supply base is not in good shape going into this," said Sheldon Klein, a Troy, Mich., based attorney at law firm \uc2 Butzel Long\uc2 , who represents auto-industry clients.\par \par \uc2 The impact on suppliers is limited at this stage of the strike, but if more car factories go offline, parts orders will dry up and payments from automakers will stop.\par \par \uc2 If that happens, the damage could ripple through the supply chain, which industry experts divide into tiers. The tier-one suppliers tend to be large international companies, such as Robert Bosch and \uc2 Magna International\uc2 , which in turn buy from suppliers lower down the tier chain. The lower the tier, the more financially vulnerable suppliers become to sudden halts in payments.\par \par \uc2 Car companies can trim costs to survive a strike by shutting down factories or laying off workers, but suppliers struggle to do the same thing. If they lay off workers or cut off payments to raw-material suppliers, the break in cash flow runs the risk of putting suppliers lower down the chain out of business.\par \par \uc2 "There is a weakness in the underpart of the supply chain that has people worried," said Ann-Marie Uetz, a Detroit-based lawyer at \uc2 Foley & Lardner\uc2  who works with automotive suppliers.\par \par \uc2 -- Sean McLain\par \par \uc2 4. CLUES IN UAW RHETORIC\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain has vowed to do things differently this time. He wasn't kidding.\par \par \uc2 This round of talks has been a series of firsts for many in Detroit -- from the union's unorthodox strike strategy to its airing, and then disparaging, of the companies' bargaining offers publicly.\par \par \uc2 Fain, who was elected in spring in a surprise upset to the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's establishment, has laced his speeches with verbal attacks that have taken auto executives aback. He seems to be everywhere, regularly holding videoconferences and making the rounds on the news talk-show circuit. It is a stunning departure for a union that has been historically guarded and tight-lipped around contract talks, especially with the press.\par \par \uc2 In turn, the CEOs of \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 GM\uc2  have also taken to the airwaves to throw their own barbs, and the volley of accusations has at times turned vicious. Trying to preempt the union, the companies have also publicized their offers, an attempt at appealing to auto workers directly.\par \par \uc2 In the past, negotiations were highly secretive, behind-closed-door events. Even the most minor developments would rarely leak, and the refrain from both the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and the companies was "we don't bargain in the press."\par \par \uc2 This unusually public war of words between the two sides has turned \uc2 UAW\uc2  talks into a must-see drama that is unfolding live on television networks and social media.\par \par \uc2 In Fain, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  for the first time has a leader directly elected by its members -- not by a group of local union leaders -- and he has a reinvigorated labor movement at his back. Fain and his lieutenants have also decided to ditch the traditional pattern-bargaining method of reaching a deal with one automaker first, followed by the other two. Instead, he is holding parallel talks with all three to keep the pressure on simultaneously.\par \par \uc2 Fain is keeping everyone on edge, and how the \uc2 UAW\uc2  continues to negotiate could impact on the U.S. auto industry for years to come.\par \par \uc2 -- Christina Rogers\par \par \uc2 5. NEW CARS ON DEALERSHIP LOTS\par \par \uc2 The auto industry has been recovering from more than two years of severe new-vehicle shortages, which nearly wiped out dealerships' inventories and resulted in American car shoppers paying top dollar.\par \par \uc2 The factory disruptions could send the market right back into a vehicle famine, but not right away. \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have rebuilt their depleted stocks more quickly than most other automakers, even though their inventories remain well below historical norms.\par \par \uc2 The bigger problem will be in the service department. Cutting off the supply of parts from striking distribution centers will hit dealerships almost immediately, and send them scrambling to find backup options. In 2019, when all of \uc2 GM\uc2 's U.S. operations were out on strike, dealers said they had to turn away service customers for a lack of parts.\par \par \uc2 For now, availability on the lots of the Detroit brands, such as Chevrolet, Cadillac, Ford, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram, should hold up for a while, unless more assembly plants go on strike.\par \par \uc2 Some shutdowns will sting more than others, though. Las Vegas car dealer Steve Olliges cringed last week when he learned that a suburban Detroit factory where the Ford Bronco is built would be one of the first three factories hit by the walkout. Olliges is one of the nation's top-selling Bronco dealers, and he already can't keep up with demand for the SUV.\par \par \uc2 "I'm concerned because dealers don't have enough as it is," he said. "It's going to hurt."\par \pa