\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Immigration Rebound Aids Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amara Omeokwe and Michelle Hackman \par \uc2 849 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy's prospects of a soft landing are getting a boost from an unexpected source: a historic rise in immigration.\par \par \uc2 The inflow of foreign-born workers, which had slowed to a trickle in the years up to and including the pandemic, is now rising briskly as the U.S. catches up on a backlog of visa applications and the Biden administration accelerates work permits.\par \par \uc2 This past week, it said it would offer work permits to 470,000 Venezuelan migrants. The effect of that action won't be immediate. Still, it is one of a series of executive actions that has had the effect of boosting the inflow of foreign-born workers.\par \par \uc2 That's helping ease labor shortages and wage and price pressure. While that alone doesn't remove the risk of recession, it makes it a bit easier for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to bring inflation down without a significant rise in unemployment -- a so-called soft landing.\par \par \uc2 This year, average monthly growth in the foreign-born labor force is about 65,000 higher compared with 2022 on a seasonally adjusted basis, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  found. After plunging at the start of the pandemic, the size of the foreign-born labor force has rebounded, nearing 32 million people in August.\par \par \uc2 Foreign-born workers' share of the labor force reached 18% in 2022, the high going back to 1996, according to the Labor Department. It has climbed further this year to an average of 18.5% through August, not adjusted for seasonal variation.\par \par \uc2 The jump in the share of foreign-born workers in the labor force reflects an easing in immigration backlogs that accumulated during the Trump administration and at the onset of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 U.S. consulates around the world shut down when the pandemic broke out and, because of staff attrition and local country restrictions, took longer than most offices in the U.S. to reopen.\par \par \uc2 It took several years to reopen consulates to their prepandemic capacity; some posts are now dealing with yearslong waits for visa appointments.\par \par \uc2 By 2022, though, the U.S. granted more than a million work visas, hitting a 25-year high, according to an analysis of government figures by USAFacts, a nonpartisan data provider. It issued nearly 500,000 green cards to immigrants moving to the U.S. permanently, the highest total since 2018, government data show.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the administration has made unprecedented use of a little-known immigration power known as humanitarian parole to quickly and legally let in hundreds of thousands of people from places such as Ukraine, Afghanistan and Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 All of this is making a palpable difference to employers who have struggled to find and retain workers.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, Veselka, a Ukrainian diner in Manhattan, was so short on cooks and wait staff that owner Jason Birchard was ready to cut the restaurant's hours.\par \par \uc2 Then last year, the war in Ukraine broke out. The Biden administration launched a program to sponsor Ukrainian refugees to live and work temporarily in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Birchard, who is third-generation Ukrainian American, immediately looked into the new program. He said he thought it was the right thing to do but also hoped he could find some new cooks.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Birchard has sponsored 10 Ukrainians, mostly extended family of his existing employees, and eight work at his restaurant.\par \par \uc2 "One of my biggest challenges postpandemic was hiring. Not so anymore," he said. "It's been a win-win for me."\par \par \uc2 Starting in 2021, the demand for workers picked up rapidly. But employers found many workers they had laid off had moved, retired, changed industries or were too wary of Covid to go back to work.\par \par \uc2 By March 2022, the number of vacant jobs surged to a historic high of 12 million, double the nearly six million unemployed, according to the Labor Department. Openings have since fallen to a still-high 8.8 million in July, roughly 50% more than the unemployed.\par \par \uc2 The falloff in migrant inflows compounded the shortage. A study by the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco\uc2  found that declines in net international migration raised the ratio of job vacancies to the unemployed by roughly 5 percentage points between 2017 and 2021.\par \par \uc2 By 2022, increasing migration had lowered the ratio by 6 percentage points, the analysis found.\par \par \uc2 One reason immigration has helped the supply of labor is that a greater proportion of newcomers join the labor force. Since early 2020, the foreign-born participation rate, now roughly 67%, has grown above its prepandemic average by 1.5 percentage points, while that for native-born Americans remains 0.5 point below, Goldman found.\par \par \uc2 Giovanni Peri, an economist and director of the Global Migration Center at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , said that while increased immigration is helping ease labor-market tightness, that is "because there was a backlog." He said more lasting relief to worker shortages in some industries requires a more deliberate policy change, "which, as of now, doesn't seem to be there."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich341\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal5115
