\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Trump Aims for Union Vote as Party's Rhetoric Shifts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Leary and Julie Bykowicz \par \uc2 704 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Enough union members voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016 to help him win the White House. Now, with nearly 13,000 auto workers on strike at three U.S. factories, the Republican former president is trying to set the stage for a sequel.\par \par \uc2 In Detroit next week, Trump plans to address hundreds of union workers, including those from the auto industry, as he attempts to siphon off support for President Biden, the self-declared most pro-union president in history. The two men are on track for a rematch of their 2020 battle as Trump holds a commanding lead over his GOP rivals.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have made gains among white, working-class voters -- a group that includes many in organized labor -- who in previous generations had voted mostly for Democrats. Meanwhile, support among Americans for organized labor is on the rise, polls show, making attacks on unions less politically attractive.\par \par \uc2 With Trump's populism leading the way, a new crop of Republican lawmakers are increasingly siding with workers over corporations they criticize for taking left-leaning stances on social issues and pursuing profits in China.\par \par \uc2 Trump has voiced support for the striking members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  and urged them to pressure union leadership to endorse him, an unlikely prospect. Like other Republicans, he has chosen his words carefully, blaming the push by Biden and Democrats to promote electric vehicles. Auto workers fear the transition from gasoline-powered cars will lead to fewer jobs.\par \par \uc2 "He's hitting a chord with some union workers," Michigan State Rep. Mike McFall, a Democrat whose district includes part of Detroit, said of Trump. "There's this view that Democrats have moved away from being the party of the people and the workers. I don't personally believe that's true, but it is a perception a lot of people have."\par \par \uc2 Biden has sided with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  -- some of whose members walked off the job Friday at assembly plants in Michigan, Ohio and Missouri of three different automakers. He has promoted electric vehicles as good for the environment and to spur domestic manufacturing as China has become an EV powerhouse.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  backed Biden's 2020 presidential bid. It hasn't yet made a 2024 endorsement. \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain said Biden needs to address concerns about the auto industry's transition to all-electric vehicles, including worries about job security, pay and organizing.\par \par \uc2 So far Biden has resisted calls by Democrats in the states where the strikes are taking place to travel to the picket line.\par \par \uc2 Republican Sen. J.D. Vance of Ohio has expressed support for the workers, some of whom are on strike in his state. The walkout "offers the \uc2 UAW\uc2  an opportunity to safeguard their future against an existential threat: President Biden's forced transition to electric vehicles," he wrote in an opinion piece published Tuesday in the Toledo Blade.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , a Republican in Missouri, another state where some \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers are on strike, said in a social-media post that "auto workers deserve a raise."\par \par \uc2 In 2016, Trump earned a level of support from union members that no Republican had reached since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , exit polls show, helping him narrowly capture critical states such as Pennsylvania, Michigan and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Democrats point to differences from seven years ago. Trump as president had a record of hurting unions and siding against workers, they argue. And the labor movement itself hassincebecomemore robust and orderly, suggesting its leadersmight have an easier time than in 2016 persuading members to vote for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 "Trump trying to suggest he's on the side of union voters is laughable," said \uc2 Steve Rosenthal\uc2 , a Democratic political strategist in the labor movement for 40 years. "On every single front, his administration was about undermining workers and attacking unions."\par \par \uc2 As president, Trump pushed through tax breaks for corporations and made appointments to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  that unions say tipped rulings in favor of employers. Trump earned the support of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  for the renegotiated \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 , but he failed to get an infrastructure bill passed, something Biden achieved.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230921ej9l00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
