\par\par \b \uc2 CEO's Exit Followed Groping Complaint\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Glazer and Theo Francis \par \uc2 1,120 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kiwi Camara\uc2 , a Harvard Law School prodigy turned technology entrepreneur, drew attention last week when he resigned from software supplier \uc2 CS Disco\uc2 , walking away from stock options once valued at nearly $110 million.\par \par \uc2 Current and former employees now say he abruptly quit as chief executive after the small company's board began investigating allegations that he groped a young female employee.\par \par \uc2 The alleged incident, at a Sept. 6 dinner with staff, triggered an investigation by outside lawyers, some of the employees said. It came over a year after a \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  employee had made a complaint about Camara's behavior around young female employees.\par \par \uc2 Camara didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Camara, 39 years old, resigned Sunday, Sept. 10, a few days after the board opened its investigation into the sexual assault allegation. Camara forfeited an award of stock options that had made him one of the highest-paid CEOs of 2022. It was the latest chapter in an unusual career that began when Camara graduated at age 19 from law school.\par \par \uc2 CS Disco\uc2 , a niche supplier of software for lawyers, gave little explanation for its leader's sudden departure, saying it didn't stem from policy or operational disagreements. Employees said they learned of his exit during a company meeting Sept. 11, where leaders wished Camara the best.\par \par \uc2 In a news release that day, venture capitalist Krishna Srinivasan, the board's chairman and a director since 2013, said: "We are grateful to Kiwi for his vision, passion, and dedication to Disco over the past decade."\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  referred back to the company's statements in the news release and a related securities filing. Srinivasan didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal spoke with more than a dozen current and former \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  employees as well as other people familiar with the matter for this article.\par \par \uc2 Camara co-founded \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  in 2013 -- when he was 29 -- to develop software used by lawyers to sort through documents. The company hired hundreds of employees, whom Camara called "Discovians," and the CEO regularly hosted alcohol-fueled social events for them.\par \par \uc2 On Sept. 6, the CEO participated in a companywide happy hour in a food hall in the Austin, Texas, building that houses the company's main offices. At the event, Camara was encouraging employees to take shots of tequila.\par \par \uc2 After the happy hour, Camara invited a small group of employees to dinner at the nearby Peacock Mediterranean Grill. At the restaurant, the drinking continued and Camara pushed roasted meat into the face of one young employee, telling her to eat it "like an animal."\par \par \uc2 Camara groped the woman, who was visibly uncomfortable, and tried to coax her to return to his condo, some of the employees said. Employees intervened to stop the CEO and the woman left. The incident was reported by employees to \uc2 CS Disco\uc2 's head of human resources the same night.\par \par \uc2 The next day, the law firm Cooley opened an investigation on behalf of the board and interviewed people who attended the dinner. It couldn't be learned what the investigation determined.\par \par \uc2 Cooley previously did legal work for \uc2 CS Disco\uc2 , including advising on its initial public stock offering. A Cooley spokesman declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't the first time that a \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  employee had complained about Camara's behavior with young female employees.\par \par \uc2 The CEO hired some female receptionists only after he reviewed photos of them, a practice he didn't follow for men, according to former employees and a lawsuit filed in federal court in Austin in 2018 by a former \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  employee. Part of the suit was dismissed and the parties agreed to dismiss the rest, which alleged age and gender discrimination.\par \par \uc2 In early 2022, a \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  employee contacted the company's ethics hotline requesting an investigation into Camara's treatment of young female employees.\par \par \uc2 The company's finance chief and others reviewed the materials. An outside lawyer, also from Cooley, investigated the complaint.It couldn't be learned what the investigation found.\par \par \uc2 Several former \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  employees said that in recent years they had raised concerns about Camara's behavior to human resources, top executives and during exit interviews, and that they felt nothing changed.\par \par \uc2 In February 2022, one employee complained to human resources that the CEO surrounds himself with young female staffers and "prefers to have a house party with associates rather than a professional dinner. This is very inappropriate behavior," according to a document reviewed by the Journal. It is unclear if the company took any action.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, Camara spearheaded a program at \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  called the Emerging Leader Rotational Program, or ELRP. The company would recruit and hire about 40 new college graduates, offering them a roughly $80,000 job where they would spend several years rotating through divisions of the company.\par \par \uc2 A recent job description for the ELRP states: "Working alongside top legal minds, entrepreneurs, and technologists, you'll make a meaningful impact while deepening your skills with one-to-one attention from our leaders and coaches."\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  managers familiar with the ELRP said Camara would socialize with the company's youngest staff. He sought to select young female ELRP associates, they said, to do rotations in the CEO's office. Weekend events for ELRP associates such as visits to wineries struck managers as wasteful while executives were urging belt tightening.\par \par \uc2 A former ELRP associate said the boss would strongly encourage his visitors to try his brandy or cognac and smoke cigars with him. Camara generally wasn't easy to refuse. "He'd say stuff like, 'I'll fire you if you don't do things my way,' " this person said.\par \par \uc2 Camara was born in the Philippines in 1984. The family later moved to Honolulu. He entered \uc2 Hawaii Pacific University\uc2  at 14 and graduated in two years.\par \par \uc2 Camara enrolled at Harvard Law School at 17. In his first year there, Camara drew criticism for posting course outlines that used a racist term for Black people. He apologized, saying he couldn't explain why he used the term, the Harvard Crimson reported.\par \par \uc2 When he took \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  public in July 2021, Camara wrote a letter to investors where he said: "We are committed to making Disco the kind of place that we would like to tell other people we were involved in building, the kind of place where we would love for our kids to work."\par \par \uc2 The shares more than doubled after the IPO, but the stock has since slumped and \uc2 CS Disco\uc2  is now valued at around $400 million.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Jim Oberman contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230921ej9l0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
