\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Auto Union Strike's Demands Drive Wedge in Biden's Economic Policy\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Harrison \par \uc2 837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike at U.S. carmakers exposes a conflict at the heart of the Biden administration's economic policy that could be difficult to resolve.\par \par \uc2 On the one hand, President Biden promotes new investment in clean technology and electric vehicles to bring manufacturing back to U.S. shores. On the other, he celebrates labor unions and supports their demands for higher wages.\par \par \uc2 Big wage increases will make it harder for the U.S. to build an electric-vehicle industry that can challenge China's dominance, said \uc2 Willy Shih\uc2 , a management professor at \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This puts the administration really in a fix," Shih said. "You can take the side of labor and say, 'OK, let's raise everybody's costs.' But then what's the long-term competitiveness of the domestic industry?"\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 , some of whose members walked off the job on Friday, wants a pay raise of more than 30% over four years, 32-hour workweeks, an end to the use of temporary workers and a reversal of some concessions made during the 2007-09 recession when \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler, now part of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , went bankrupt. Among them: restoring cost-of-living adjustments so that wages rise with inflation.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  base wages have risen an average 6% since the last contract in 2019, the union said. Its members have also received lump-sum payments and profit-sharing. In that time, vehicle prices are up almost 23% and overall consumer prices 19%, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 The union points to fat profit margins that the Big Three -- \uc2 GM\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  -- earned as a limited supply caused by parts shortages and strong household demand pushed up prices. Companies say those profits are intended to help transition to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Still, union workers' earnings including benefits of roughly $60 per hour, according to the companies, exceeds the $55 average at nonunion plants and $45 at nonunion \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , according to \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts.\par \par \uc2 The stakes go beyond wages. Both the automakers and the union are trying to set the terms for the industry's reinvention in the age of electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  holds 60% of the U.S. electric-vehicle market, compared with 11% for the Big Three, according to \uc2 Cox Automotive\uc2 . Their overall U.S. market share has fallen to 40% from 45% in 2015.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. lags well behind China in electric vehicles. China started subsidizing EVs more than a decade ago, and now accounts for more than half of all EVs on the road, according to the \uc2 International Energy Agency\uc2 . Earlier this year, China edged out Japan as the world's top auto exporter. China's BYD was the world's second-biggest seller of EVs, behind \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , while China's \uc2 GAC Aion\uc2  was third.\par \par \uc2 Raising labor costs by as much as the \uc2 UAW\uc2  wants would make it harder to close the gap with China, said Tu Le, an auto industry consultant with long experience in China.\par \par \uc2 "If the contract is anywhere close to those numbers, then maybe at least at the below-$50,000 price point we'll be driving a lot of Chinese cars," he said.\par \par \uc2 Biden said Friday that the transition to EVs should be a "win-win" for union workers and companies. "Workers deserve a fair share of the benefits they helped create for an enterprise," he said.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials say it is possible to have both high union wages and competitive automakers, especially now that profits are soaring. Directing some of those profits to wages and enlisting workers to find productivity improvements could help achieve both, said a senior administration official.\par \par \uc2 Biden's signature economic-policy achievement, the Inflation Reduction Act, enacted last year, offers tax credits of up to $7,500 for EV buyers, loans for clean-energy projects, incentives for recycling EV batteries and tax credits for clean-energy technologies such as hydrogen and carbon capture.\par \par \uc2 EVs and batteries accounted for roughly 87% of all automaker investment in North America over the past two years, said \uc2 Alan Amici\uc2 , president of the Center for Automotive Research, a Michigan think tank. But much of that has been in the South, where wages are lower and unions weaker.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 , meanwhile, says IRA subsidies have allowed companies to undercut workers. In June, \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain expressed outrage at the Biden administration's $9.2 billion loan to a Ford joint venture to build battery plants in Kentucky and Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 "The switch to electric engine jobs, battery production and other EV manufacturing cannot become a race to the bottom," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 Ford spokeswoman Melissa Miller said the plants "will pay competitive wages and benefits." Employees will be able to choose whether to join a union, "a right that Ford fully respects and supports," she said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  so far hasn't endorsed Biden's re-election. Fain has said Biden would have to earn his backing.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich296\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4440
