\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Shawn Fain's Economic Reality Test\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) \uc2 strike is going on day five, and President Shawn Fain has rejected the latest offer from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  of a 21% pay raise over four years. He keeps citing corporate "greed" like a mantra. Yet it was telling this weekend when he refused to discuss the union's impact on future job creation.\par \par \uc2 Asked on \uc2 CBS\uc2 's Face the Nation how he would make the case to Detroit auto makers to invest "in more expensive union shops rather than move to these right-to-work states," Mr. Fain ducked like a politician. \uc2 UAW\uc2  worker pay, he said, shouldn't be compared "to how pitiful \uc2 Tesla\uc2  pays their workers and other companies pay their workers."\par \par \uc2 He added: "Most of these workers in those companies are scraping to get by so that greedy CEOs and greedy people like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  can build more rocket ships and shoot herself in outer space." Mr. Fain can criticize Mr. Musk all he wants, but \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is the chief competitor to the Detroit auto makers in the government-mandated electric-vehicle race. That's the reality corporate CEOs have to consider, even if Mr. Fain ignores it.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  disclosures, the median worker at \uc2 Tesla\uc2  earned $34,084 last year in total compensation, compared to $80,034 at \uc2 GM\uc2  and $74,691 at Ford. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said its average worker earned 64,328 euros ($68,683). These internal calculations include non-production level workers as well as employees located in other countries, which in the case of \uc2 Tesla\uc2  includes China.\par \par \uc2 Yet the U.S. accounts for a larger share of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's workforce than it does for Ford or \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . A large reason for the pay disparity is Detroit's higher retirement costs. Ford reported a $1.2 billion cost last year for its U.S. worker retirement benefits, largely defined-benefit pensions and retiree medical care, according to its securities filings.\par \par \uc2 Detroit auto makers scrapped defined-benefit pensions and retiree medical care for new workers in 2007 as they lurched toward insolvency. But Mr. Fain wants to restore these benefits for all workers, which would create enormous new unfunded liabilities and obligations. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  offers its workers 401(k)s with a contribution match of up to $3,000, which keeps a lid on future legacy costs.\par \par \uc2 We understand the financial blow that the inflation of the last two years has been for workers, and that the union wants this next contract to recover lost ground. But Ford CEO \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  said last week that the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's demands would more than double his company's union-related labor costs and drive it into bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 "The average pay would be nearly $300,000 for a four-day work week," he noted. Mr. Fain insisted over the weekend that the auto makers could afford such higher pay since labor makes up a small share of a car's costs.\par \par \uc2 Yet labor is one of the few variables auto makers can control, which is why \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and foreign auto makers have tried to avoid unionization by expanding in U.S. right-to-work states. Mr. Fain is vindicating their decision.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Pensions vs. Jobs in Detroit" -- WSJ Sept. 26, 2023)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230919ej9j00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
