\par\par \b \uc2 Car Union President Threatens To Widen Strikes --- Wages remain focus as investors size up potential hits to companies' earnings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Felton and Nora Eckert \par \uc2 872 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  threatened Monday to widen strikes against Detroit's carmakers, while Wall Street analysts tallied potential hits to the companies' bottom line from wage increases that now seem certain.\par \par \uc2 Tensions remained high four days after \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers went on strike at three U.S. factories. The union and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have been trading barbs over a company proposal to possibly shutter 18 U.S. facilities as part of a potential labor agreement.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  presented the proposal to the \uc2 UAW\uc2  before the company's current labor agreement with the union expired Thursday. The plan could reduce the number of parts and distribution facilities the company maintains in the U.S., but the automaker said it wasn't aimed at cutting jobs.\par \par \uc2 The automaker separately offered to repurpose a currently idled assembly plant in Illinois. The factory, which made Jeep Cherokees, was shuttered in February and resulted in indefinite layoffs of 1,350 people, some of whom have taken jobs elsewhere in the company.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain criticized the company's handling of the proposal, saying that \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  was using workers at the Belvidere, Ill., plant as a bargaining chip in the talks.\par \par \uc2 Whether the proposal is still on the table couldn't be learned, though the company said Monday it was committed to identifying a solution for the facility.\par \par \uc2 About 12,700 factory workers picketed for the fourth day at a \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  pickup factory near Detroit, a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  Jeep plant in Toledo, Ohio, and a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  commercial-van factory near St. Louis. Fain opted for an unusual, targeted strike at each company instead of a full walkout of the union's 146,000 auto workers.\par \par \uc2 In a video the \uc2 UAW\uc2  shared Monday night, Fain said he would release a new round of plant targets at noon on Friday if the companies don't move enough on their proposals.\par \par \uc2 "We're not waiting around, and we're not messing around," he said.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders are worried about layoffs and plant closures as automakers gradually shift their lineups to electric vehicles. The union is fighting to organize EV-assembly plants and battery factories popping up across the country.\par \par \uc2 Mark Stewart\uc2 , \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's North American chief operating officer, said Saturday that the company's proposal for the 18 facilities would largely help modernize parts and distribution plants operated by its Mopar brand, which sells genuine parts and accessories.\par \par \uc2 "It's not about reducing jobs," Stewart said in a call Saturday. "It's about consolidating, and in cases where we own it, selling underutilized real estate so the company can compete in an electrified vehicle world."\par \par \uc2 In a sign of the rising political importance of the strike, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is expected to travel to Detroit next week for a speech before hundreds of union members, an aide said, as he ramps up attempts to attract rank-and-file union workers ahead of the 2024 campaign. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has yet to endorse a candidate in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's 5.4 million- square-foot North American headquarters campus was among the list of facilities targeted for possible closure, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 The facility is the largest office space in the country after the Pentagon, according to the company's website. It has a dedicated exit ramp on I-75 in suburban Detroit, and at one point, about 15,000 employees worked out of the facility.\par \par \uc2 The company had previously said it was evaluating its real-estate footprint and has gone to a hybrid work model. It isn't guaranteed that facilities would be sold under a labor agreement.\par \par \uc2 CNBC reported earlier on details of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's proposal.\par \par \uc2 Discussions around wages have taken center stage in the negotiations between the companies-- which are bargaining independently with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  in parallel talks-- over new four-year contracts for 146,000 auto workers.\par \par \uc2 The companies have made offers of around 20% wage increases over four years, while the union has been pressing for a bump in the mid-30% range. The union also wants cost-of-living adjustments added into base pay, while carmakers have offered lump-sum payments to protect against inflation.\par \par \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  analysts estimate that the companies' proposals would add an estimated $700 million to $1.2 billion in costs to each company over the four-year life of the contract. Those costs would hit $1.7 billion to $2.4 billion under the union's mid-30% ask, plus cost-of-living adjustments.\par \par \uc2 Some workers have expressed concerns about how they will be financially supported if their plant goes down as a ripple effect of walkouts at other facilities. A union official confirmed Monday that just like striking workers, nonstriking workers who are laid off will be supported by $500 weekly pay.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  signaled some progress in its discussions with Ford over the weekend, saying it had "reasonably productive conversations" with the company. Ford said it is "committed to reaching an agreement with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  that rewards our workers and allows Ford to invest in the future."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230919ej9j0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
