\par\par \b \uc2 C-Suite Strategies (A Special Report) --- How Do You Build a Corporate Culture In a Hybrid or Remote Workplace? It isn't easy, but there are ways for colleagues who aren't physically together to feel connected to the mission. Readers share their strategies.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Demetria Gallegos \par \uc2 3,152 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A manager I worked with during the pandemic began every weekly staff meeting with a different perplexing question: Do you believe in ghosts? How often do you wash your hair? If it was the 1940s and America was in the midst of World War II, how would you contribute to the war effort?\par \par \uc2 The first minutes of the meeting were spent with each of us sharing a memory or advice or a spy fantasy, often amid laughter and appreciative comments. As a newcomer to the group, it gave me a better understanding of who these colleagues were, even though everyone was joining the call from home. It's also the only part of those meetings that I can recall today.\par \par \uc2 In that sense, question time worked to build a team and boost morale when many of us were facing some adversity at home or professional burnout. So now that remote and hybrid work have taken hold, and more workers report feeling disconnected from the mission of their employer, leaders are struggling with how to create and sustain corporate culture when workers come into the office only some of the time, or not at all. So we asked Wall Street Journal readers -- which measures have worked and which haven't?\par \par \uc2 Here are some of their ideas.\par \par \uc2 Simon says, 'Let's connect'\par \par \uc2 We have established a team of culture champions across our globally distributed team. They organize in-person and virtual activities, polls, chats and other connection points in each of three geographies. For me, this has restored a welcome reminder of the organic "we're in this together" spirit that emerged during those crazy times when everything wasvirtual, from competitive trivia nights to coffee chats to music to work by (song requests encouraged).\par \par \uc2 We especially try to foster connections between colleagues who don't normally work together. Even though we're all in the same function (marketing), we have a lot of recent joiners who still don't know what the subfunctions do, so informal get-to-know-you activities foster empathy and business understanding. We balance virtual with in-person activities: Last week, I was the caller for "Simon Says" on the front lawn of our largest office in California. Employee engagement scores for autonomy and flexibility are at the highest I've seen, so I take that as a sign we're on the right track.\par \par \uc2 -- Cristin Balog, San Leandro, Calif.\par \par \uc2 A consistent schedule\par \par \uc2 Consistency of schedule is key. I telework Monday and Friday and keep to it, pending in-person needs. Since my job requires so much collaboration, knowing when I'll be in the office helps my team know when to plan in-person versus virtual meetings. My employer requested three days a week but delegated authority for me to designate my telework days. This flexibility is a boost in job satisfaction and allows me to choose what works best for my duties.\par \par \uc2 What has worked well in hybrid/remote work includes flexibility and adaptation to maintain mission effectiveness, good collaborative tools, consistent messaging of policies and priorities across the staff regardless of their location or work status, managing expectations, and bringing all workers in for group quarterly gatherings to foster greater team unity.\par \par \uc2 What hasn't worked includes requiring workers to return to the office full time with sparse reasoning, inconsistent communication between hybrid/remote and in-person workers, and elevating workers who have been showing up in-person over those who are hybrid/remotely performing at a high standard.\par \par \uc2 -- Reed Schafer, Rapid City, Mich.\par \par \uc2 The person, then their work\par \par \uc2 If your culture is just ping pong and bean bags, then you have bigger challenges than remote work. But if the culture is about people and how you support each other, remote work isn't an insurmountable challenge.\par \par \uc2 I try to create connection with my teams. Make sure people are doing work they feel good about. Make sure they feel supported. What are they concerned with? Connect people on the team with each other if that will help solve a problem. Listen to your teammates. Find out who they are and what's important to them. Lead your check-in with some connection time to show your people that you care more about them than the work. A good team will take this support and deliver the best work you've ever seen.\par \par \uc2 One of the challenges of remote work is the opportunity for problems to be kept quiet. That is more easily overcome by connection than it is by proximity.\par \par \uc2 People also need a few contacts they stay in touch with just for personal reasons. Work might be a small part of the conversation. Some of my most valuable supporters have been people with whom 90% of the conversation is focused on weekends, the kids or their spouse.\par \par \uc2 -- Jor Molchan, Boston\par \par \uc2 Pick up the phone\par \par \uc2 You have to reach out. Don't wait for someone to call you. On many occasions, I realized it had been weeks or even months since I had spoken with someone.\par \par \uc2 I think people get hung up on ego and status issues, which lead them to think "Hey, l'm important -- let them call me." That only leads to losing touch. Let's face it -- the pivot to remote work has changed the workplace fundamentally. Now, you really have to make an effort to stay in touch with people whom you formerly saw daily or weekly. I am always glad to speak to and hear from current and former colleagues. Often they say, "I was about to reach out to you" or "I was wondering what you were up to."\par \par \uc2 -- Kenneth Abbott, New York\par \par \uc2 Remote from the start\par \par \uc2 I decided nine years ago to start a completely virtual company, and we've been operating as such ever since. Without commuting time, we've reduced mental frustration and wear-and-tear on employees, increasing time for them to focus on clients and their own mental and physical well-being.\par \par \uc2 We don't track paid time off. Today's workers are always reachable and always "on," but that can lead to burnout. Everyone rejuvenates differently, so to truly empower an employee, you need to allow them to take time whenever they want. Treat them as the professionals they are. We have never had an issue with employees abusing the unlimited PTO policy.\par \par \uc2 Without the need to hire locally, we can hire the strongest professionals regardless of where they live, which increases diversity. We limit virtual meetings but make sure the entire company meets once a week. Video is optional except for that weekly all-hands meeting, to help foster connection. Twice a year we hold in-person off-sites to ensure we don't lose the ability to strategize and build important connections.\par \par \uc2 We also create space in our budget for in-person events throughout the year, and we hold semiannual virtual happy hours that focus on specific events like cooking and yoga.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes, we open these up to our clients. Virtual gives us the flexibility to do this, and it's always a nice way to bond with our employees and clients and partners.\par \par \uc2 We laminate and print out our annual goals and corporate values, and mail home books and other important culture messages so employees feel connected to the company.\par \par \uc2 It's my view that companies struggling with the hybrid/remote era are often resisting change, instead of focusing on the absolute real benefits of a remote and virtual company -- more employee empowerment and trust, lower costs and more value for clients.\par \par \uc2 -- Ray Kimble, Bristow, Va.\par \par \uc2 Outcomes, not input\par \par \uc2 For me, the flexibility to work when and where I want has been life-changing. I think the secret to keeping team morale high is the high level of trust my colleagues and I enjoy. We're judged by outcomes, not how many hours we're in front of our keyboards. This feeling of trust motivates us to give our best in return.\par \par \uc2 -- Connor Murphy, Portland, Ore.\par \par \uc2 Volunteer together\par \par \uc2 We are a 70-person firm based out of one office. Our culture is extremely important to us, and it is one of our largest recruiting and retention tools. We began offering work from home during Covid, and we continue to offer flexibility as to whether employees want to work at the office, remotely or use a hybrid model.\par \par \uc2 It is much harder, if not impossible, to re-create the short interactions that we used to have, such as when we arrived at a meeting early, or lingering in the kitchen while heating up lunch. So we have intentionally offered more companywide outings during business hours to promote teamwork. They come at a cost -- both the cost of the events and the lost billable hours -- but we believe strongly that the connections forged between employees make our teams more efficient and able to communicate more easily.\par \par \uc2 One example is our paid Volunteer Hours program. In celebration of our 15-year anniversary, we have a goal to perform 1,500 volunteer hours (during regular working hours) as a company this year. We have given each employee three paid volunteer days and we are taking two additional paid workdays to have companywide Days of Service. The employees have taken the initiative to promote various volunteer opportunities and ask their co-workers to join them.\par \par \uc2 Every month, employees talk about their experiences at a companywide meeting, share pictures and promote volunteer opportunities. More often than not, the employees are volunteering as groups of five or 10 people, and they are building connections that we believe will make our company culture stronger.\par \par \uc2 -- Sarah Emily Colby, Portland, Maine\par \par \uc2 As simple as X, Y and Z\par \par \uc2 It is much easier to set office rules than it is to build culture; I believe the gap between the two is a key barometer of a business's potential success or stagnation. When I talk to our teams about projects or programs, I tell them why we are doing it and how it is going to help someone or add value. When I'm asking someone to dig deeper or expand their efforts, I take the time to explain how that will help them skill up and how it will help their career progress.\par \par \uc2 People need to hear that every single day on every single call: "The purpose of this work is X. We need you to add this to your job because it will help us Y and it will help you Z." It doesn't matter if you do this on Zoom or in a conference room full of people. It isn't where people are, it is what you convey to them.\par \par \uc2 We try to facilitate a fly-in among key teams quarterly. We need to see each other to really focus our minds on each other's needs, and on the needs of our members and clients.\par \par \uc2 Our fly-ins help us talk more deeply about purpose and distill our culture, which provides the glue for our mission, values and strategies. And if you get it right, you can be flexible on where a person works and how they work instead of trying to set rigid rules.\par \par \uc2 -- Andy Polk, Reston, Va.\par \par \uc2 It's about gratitude\par \par \uc2 Don't underestimate practicing gratitude at work to build culture. Daily, help team members feel valued and acknowledged for what they do well. Start meetings by inviting people to write down three things they're grateful for or to acknowledge another team member who has helped them recently. Infuse gratitude into your culture in more significant ways, too.\par \par \uc2 Here's one of my favorite examples I've organized: Provide a booklet for each team member in the meeting and ask everyone to fill out one line of gratitude or a kind note about their colleagues. Each team member gets to take the compiled booklet as a reminder of their contributions -- which creates positive feelings well beyond the meeting.\par \par \uc2 -- Shanna Hocking, Merion Station, Pa.\par \par \uc2 Set the agenda in person\par \par \uc2 We begin each fiscal year with a week of in-person meetings. We have very clear parameters around performance expectations, so workers feel they are working toward corporate goals because the system is designed to reward that kind of work.\par \par \uc2 Some people will have to work fully in-person, and resentment is a natural byproduct of that. But to give everyone a bit of extra flexibility, we allow four-day weeks twice a month during the summer for everyone (generally, Fridays off twice a month). We provide career-growth opportunities as well, so people currently stuck in a fully in-person role could be promoted up to a role that allows more flexibility.\par \par \uc2 -- Tom Hall, Newport News, Va.\par \par \uc2 Care packages\par \par \uc2 We have fully embraced a remote and hybrid work environment, but building culture with new hires continues to pose a challenge. We have addressed this by engaging our team members with regular video updates from management and executives. We are also using a tool that allows our team members to better feel part of the team by having video access to see their other team members and supervisors, similar to how it would be in the office. Finally, we try to connect with our team members by sending care packages, and asking our team members to take pictures of themselves with the care package, or a sports-team shirt day that we can put together into a social-media post or company video.\par \par \uc2 -- Jack Brown, Sarasota, Fla.\par \par \uc2 Remember the families\par \par \uc2 Staying connected as a team is highly important to our collective well-being, and we do have an annual in-person get together. The rest of the time, we don't dwell on the remote part, but do dwell on the team part. As the leader, I make it a priority to celebrate small wins through our monthly all-hands Zoom call. To commemorate personal celebrations and accomplishments, we sometimes put a shout-out on \uc2 Slack\uc2  or send a card in the mail or ship a treat home.\par \par \uc2 For the holidays, we have a fun online party and the team receives interactive gifts to enjoy with their own families (last year was a DIY pizza kit).\par \par \uc2 -- Ira Gostin, Reno, Nev.\par \par \uc2 Overcommunicate and be fair\par \par \uc2 I have been a remote employee and a leader for over 10 years. Here are some of the best approaches I experienced and have practiced in this context.\par \par \uc2 As an employee: I appreciated my leadership team overcommunicating with me. Since I wasn't around the office to have spontaneous conversations about what was happening with the business, my bosses had frequent short calls with me to update me on what was going on and asking me what was happening with my function and what I needed. I was often asked for my input about potential decisions and felt like my input was taken seriously.\par \par \uc2 I was also asked often about how my personal life was going; I never felt that this was intrusive but showed care for my overall life and not just my work.\par \par \uc2 I had only two concerns: I was on the West Coast, but with the office on the East Coast, I often had to join calls at 5 a.m. And when my co-workers had food provided at meetings or were taken out to lunch, I would have appreciated a simple gesture like a gift card to help me feel valued as well.\par \par \uc2 As a leader of remote employees: I initially struggled with how to keep my team engaged and productive. So I started by asking what they wanted and needed from me, their peers and the company as a whole.\par \par \uc2 What I heard was they wanted communication via other channels than just an email. A call or even a text from me would show that I was paying attention to them and was more personal. I also made certain that they got messages from our senior leaders about some of their specific accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 Again, this helped them feel part of the larger team. I would arrange for them to present at company meetings so they got greater visibility. I would have some informal and spontaneous "coffee breaks" for which I would periodically call an employee to just check in and see how they were doing. This wasn't only a personal touch, but also helped me to see if there were any potential issues brewing.\par \par \uc2 One final strategic action I took was to pair up new employees with a remote mentor. The goal was to create a partnership where the newbie had a go-to resource for not only the work, but also the culture. It enabled people to get up to speed quicker and feel more connected.\par \par \uc2 -- Mitchell J. Nash, Longmont, Colo.\par \par \uc2 Trust me, I'm working\par \par \uc2 If employers want to build corporate culture, they must first establish goodwill with their employees. This involves trusting employees to do their job properly whether they are working in the office or at home.\par \par \uc2 I personally find I am much more productive working from home. I give it 110%, and I don't waste time with water-cooler chitchat or office politics. I also save on commuting time and expenses and wardrobe costs associated with having to come into the office each day.\par \par \uc2 One caveat to this is that I recommend employees working from home maintain a spreadsheet of all their work throughout the week. This is a CYA move in the event that a manager asks, "So what have you accomplished lately?"\par \par \uc2 -- Caroline S. Brooks, New Orleans\par \par \uc2 Meet your employer halfway\par \par \uc2 My company has gone to great lengths to ensure our hybrid work is fulfilling and enjoyable. But what has helped me the absolute most is an internal check that I am responsible for making on myself.\par \par \uc2 It's the acknowledgment of the joy and satisfaction of a healthy work/life balance. We all strive for that balance, but often, when we get it, we don't take that internal moment of gratitude to say, "Hmmm. . .I am walking my dog to the farmers market while on a Zoom call today. Today has been a good day for my balance."\par \par \uc2 Not every day or even every week has that balance, but for me, the internal acknowledgment and appreciation of when that balance is found, rather than hoping it's somewhere in the future -- that is what has made all the difference for me.\par \par \uc2 -- Jeff Schiffman, New Orleans\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Demetria Gallegos is an editor for The Wall Street Journal in New York. She can be reached at demetria.gallegos@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230918ej9i00002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
