\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Challenges for President Mount A Year Before Re-Election Bid\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sabrina Siddiqui and Tarini Parti \par \uc2 747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- September keeps getting worse for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Hours after his son was indicted last week, auto workers went on strike, walking out of plants owned by three major car companies. A possible government shutdown looms at the end of the month, and House Republicans have launched an impeachment inquiry. Gas prices are rising, and Biden's poll numbers remain underwater as he campaigns for a second term.\par \par \uc2 Biden has responded by portraying Republicans as more focused on theatrics than legislation. The White House pointed to Biden's actions in September as evidence of his leadership, citing investments in fighting cancer, the rollout of new Covid-19 boosters and dispatching top officials to Detroit to help support the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 . "The president is focused on one thing: delivering for the American people," White House spokeswoman Olivia Dalton said.\par \par \uc2 Here is a closer look at the biggest problems on Biden's plate:\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike\par \par \uc2 The walkout by \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers at three plants comes after the union failed to clinch new labor deals with \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Jeep\uc2 -maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  for about 146,000 U.S. factory workers.\par \par \uc2 The strike comes as Biden -- who polls show is tied in hypothetical 2024 rematches with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- tries to convince voters that the economy is on the upswing.\par \par \uc2 "It's not been a super news week for the president," said Adrian Hemond, a Democratic strategist in Michigan. Hemond said the political impact of the strike on the president will depend on how long it lasts and how that makes voters feel about the economy. "If the vibes aren't great, he will take some damage."\par \par \uc2 Son's indictment\par \par \uc2 Thursday's indictment of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  on federal gun charges was the latest development in a legal saga that has trailed the president's son and been a focal point for Republicans in Congress. The indictment sets up a high-profile prosecution of a sitting president's son as his father campaigns for a second term.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has so far remained silent on the indictment, which followed the collapse of a plea deal in late July that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and his team hoped would resolve a five-year investigation into his business dealings and taxes. Instead, President Biden will now have to face continued questions about his son's actions.\par \par \uc2 The White House has emphasized that it hasn't gotten involved in any of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's legal matters and that President Biden has never discussed them with the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Impeachment probe\par \par \uc2 Republicans are aiming to put \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's broader business dealings squarely at the center of the 2024 election. Announcing the impeachment inquiry Tuesday, Speaker Kevin McCarthy said that House Republicans "have uncovered serious and credible allegations into President Biden's conduct" related to his son's activities.\par \par \uc2 No evidence has emerged to show that the president benefited from Hunter Biden's endeavors or that he wielded government authority to favor them. But McCarthy has faced pressure from his right flank to launch an impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say Republicans are trying to muddy the waters in an election that will likely see a rematch between Biden and Trump, who faces four criminal indictments and is the front-runner for the GOP nomination. The White House, which has spent months preparing for the likelihood of an impeachment inquiry under House Republican control, decried the move as an "evidence-free goose chase."\par \par \uc2 Funding fight\par \par \uc2 The threat of a government shutdown looms if Congress and the White House can't agree on a way to continue funding after Sept. 30.\par \par \uc2 House conservatives are seeking deeper reductions in spending, calling for more border security and resisting more aid for Ukraine, which the White House wants attached to any short-term spending bill. In past shutdown fights, Republicans have usually suffered more politically than Democrats, though it is unclear how one would reverberate this time.\par \par \uc2 Gas prices, inflation\par \par \uc2 Biden has spent months touting a buoyant labor market and decline in inflation from a peak of 9.1% in June 2022 to 3% a year later. But rising gas prices could undercut part of the president's message.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices rose by 0.6% in August, the largest increase in more than a year, according to the Labor Department. More than half of the increase was due to higher gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230918ej9i00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
