\par\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2  Flipped Strike Playbook For Surprise-Attack Strategy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert, Mike Colias and Ryan Felton \par \uc2 2,195 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Over the summer, top officials at the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  gathered for frequent strike-strategy meetings around a 30-foot table in a boardroom at union headquarters, overlooking the Detroit River.\par \par \uc2 Their plan: craft an approach that would throw the auto companies into disarray.\par \par \uc2 The tradition-steeped union for decades had largely stuck to the same basic playbook in its dealings with Detroit's three automakers. Then just a few months into his job as the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's top official, Shawn Fain, the 54-year-old who came up in the union as an electrician, was prepared to throw it into the trash bin.\par \par \uc2 "This strategy will keep the companies guessing," Fain said late Thursday, on the eve of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's walkout at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The action was called after two months of negotiations failed to yield new labor deals for the union's roughly 146,000 auto members.\par \par \uc2 The strike -- which targets three assembly plants in three different states -- was the first time the 88-year-old union had staged simultaneous walkouts at all three automakers. Fain has threatened walkouts at additional plants could come, and with little notice, the longer negotiations drag on.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford negotiators met with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  for bargaining over the weekend. The union and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  agreed to resume their talks on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The unusual deployment of a rolling, slow-burn approach is designed to stymie the car companies' factory operations as a way to gain leverage for the union. It comes just as the carmakers are recovering from supply-chain dysfunction that disrupted their global output during the pandemic, illustrating the logistical havoc of sudden plant shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 For Fain, there are also risks. The novel tactic creates more complexity and confusion for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's members, and will require more discipline among workers in order to succeed, some workers and labor experts say. Unlike a traditional all-in walkout, Fain's strategy is having an uneven effect on members within an organization whose motto is solidarity.\par \par \uc2 "If you're going to take out one plant, take us all out, let us all stand together," said Brooke Jesse, who works at a Ford assembly plant in Ohio that produces commercial vehicles. "We all have to feel the pain."\par \par \uc2 Fain started his union career in Kokomo, Ind., at Chrysler 29 years ago. He was elected by a slim margin in March, under a new system in which members directly elect their leaders instead of the traditional style of local officials doing the voting. Fain campaigned on a hard break from the past, following a multiyear corruption scandal that resulted in the convictions of more than a dozen \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials, including prison time for two former union presidents.\par \par \uc2 There also was a perception that union leaders were too cozy with automaker executives, after federal prosecutors accused \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2 , since absorbed into \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , of steering worker-training money to \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials. The automaker's U.S. division in 2021 pleaded guilty to a criminal charge of conspiring to violate U.S. labor law.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after winning the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's top office, Fain shook up his team, losing some of the union's longtime staffers and bringing on new recruits with records of success in labor organizing outside the auto union.\par \par \uc2 The president's core advisers included a cohort of experienced \uc2 UAW\uc2  members who were keen to work with the new administration, including people in research and organizing who were knowledgeable about the union's history and had relationships with its locals.\par \par \uc2 There were also newcomers, especially in the communications, legal and political strategy departments. Members of this team largely had roots in the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , a big healthcare union, as well as Labor Notes, an online publication that describes itself as a voice for union activists.\par \par \uc2 "I have my own ideas, but I thought it was important to bring in people that weren't ingrained in the system," Fain told The Wall Street Journal in an August interview from the union's headquarters, known to members as Solidarity House.\par \par \uc2 Among those who became his closest advisers are Chris Brooks, an organizer and reporter who formerly wrote for Labor Notes and was a field director at the NewsGuild of New York. The NewsGuild earlier this year secured a pay increase of at least 10.6% for \uc2 New York Times\uc2  employees after a 24-hour walkout in December.\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Dictor, a partner at law firm Eisner Dictor & Lamadrid, also joined Fain's inner circle. His firm worked with the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  during its talks with the \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 , in which the union secured a new labor contract this year.\par \par \uc2 The new staffers brought a different perspective and pushed for \uc2 UAW\uc2  -- a historically guarded and insular organization -- to be more transparent and active on social media.\par \par \uc2 Since taking office, Fain has unleashed a torrent of social-media posts and regular livestreams. He stunned the car companies by talking openly about their bargaining offers, thrusting discussions that would normally be held behind closed doors into the public eye. Fain at one point made a show of tossing one of the company's proposals in the trash during a video address to members.\par \par \uc2 The new union leader has also taken a far more combative tone with the car companies, stoking speculation in Detroit earlier this summer that he might try an all-out strike at all three automakers.\par \par \uc2 Sitting in the third-floor conference room at a table covered with binders and coffee cups at their Detroit headquarters this summer, Fain and his advisers batted around a number of ideas for how they would stage a walkout.\par \par \uc2 Full strikes at all three were considered, said people with knowledge of the discussions.\par \par \uc2 Some on the leadership team, including at times Fain himself, appreciated how that approach would make a strong statement to the companies by bringing members out en masse.\par \par \uc2 Others had reservations, saying talks would likely stall in a full production shutdown, and worried the union would lose some of its leverage by going for the most aggressive plan first. There was also a simple financial calculation to consider: Such an option would rapidly drain the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's $825 million fund that it uses to pay striking workers, likely depleting it within about two months.\par \par \uc2 While that all-in option was never officially eliminated -- Fain even acknowledged publicly part of him itched to take that approach -- his team moved on to other ideas.\par \par \uc2 There was \uc2 UAW\uc2  precedent for a more-limited, strategic strike of certain factories. Striking key parts factories that serve multiple assembly plants, such as engine or transmission operations, a so-called bottleneck strike, can quickly force closures.\par \par \uc2 In 1998, a strike at plants in Flint, Mich., led to domino-effect closures of other \uc2 GM\uc2  factories that depended on their parts. The 54-day action cost the automaker about $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Some on Fain's team worried that the bottleneck approach, while putting fewer members on strike, would also be too swift and severe, capable of taking down most of an automaker's operation within days.\par \par \uc2 This would result in more members becoming unemployed even though they weren't among the striking group, fallout that the union wanted to avoid.\par \par \uc2 The group instead zeroed in on a tactic to strike individual assembly plants of each company.\par \par \uc2 A progressive, cascading strike would allow for a longer runway to keep more \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers on the job, preserving the strike fund. And the uncertainty it would sow inside the automakers' operations would give \uc2 UAW\uc2 's negotiators leverage to exert bargaining pressure, the union leaders decided.\par \par \uc2 The three plants now idled in the strike emerged as sweet spots. \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials wanted to spread the pain evenly across the three companies, people with knowledge of the plans said. Each of the three factories makes midsize pickup trucks, for example: the Ford Ranger, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's Jeep Gladiator and \uc2 GM\uc2 's Chevrolet Colorado and GMC Canyon.\par \par \uc2 "In part, these plants were chosen to balance the impact across the automakers," said Brandon Campbell, an executive board member at the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The union's message with the initial targets was to show companies it wanted to continue bargaining and reach a deal swiftly, not hit companies with the maximum pressure right after the contracts expired, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, these factories don't produce the companies' biggest moneymakers -- large pickup trucks and SUVs such as Ford's F-150 or \uc2 GM\uc2 's Cadillac Escalade -- leaving the union with those chips to play.\par \par \uc2 Over the summer, Fain surprised many by revealing that, in this round of talks, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  would break from decades of tradition by not picking one of the three automakers as its main bargaining target. He instead chose to negotiate with all three at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Historically, the union has targeted one automaker to reach a deal with first and then used it as a template for the other two. The first company is referred to as the "strike target" because if a deal can't be reached, the union strikes it first.\par \par \uc2 The automakers and union exchanged proposals in late August and into September that included fluctuating offers around wage increases, cost-of-living adjustments and time off. The companies have offered around 20% increases over four years, plus lump-sum payments of more than $16,000 in signing bonuses and inflation-protection payments. The union in recent weeks had come off its initial 40% wage-increase proposal into the mid-30% range.\par \par \uc2 It became clear the gulf between the two sides was still significant, and just after midnight Friday about 12,700 workers went on strike. The plants affected -- kept secret until just a couple of hours before the action -- are a \uc2 GM\uc2  plant near St. Louis, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  in Toledo and Ford near Detroit.\par \par \uc2 The union's more targeted approach of walkouts at key plants makes it difficult for the companies to devise contingency plans and helps stretch out the confusion for a longer period of time, labor experts and organizers say.\par \par \uc2 These surprise-tactic strikes have gained momentum with other unions, as some \uc2 UAW\uc2  staffers were aware. About two decades ago, the \uc2 Association of Flight Attendants\uc2  created its own strategy, which it called Create Havoc Around Our System, or CHAOS, and used it during negotiations with \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Over a period of several months, 24 flight attendants walked out on just seven flights without warning, causing sudden staffing complications and disruptions for the airline. Ultimately, the tactic won the union a new contract with top industry pay in return for greater productivity.\par \par \uc2 Fain's strategy will require a higher level of execution than a more-routine strike, said Thomas Kohler, a law professor at Boston College Law School. There is a risk workers who remain on the job might slow down production, which could undermine the strike strategy and lead to possible action from the companies.\par \par \uc2 "You're not only negotiating with the companies, you're negotiating with your own people," Kohler said.\par \par \uc2 The action at the three plants was more muted than some expected the union to take, though it still has led to layoffs and crimped production at other facilities over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Company executives were quick to publicly vent their frustrations with the move.\par \par \uc2 "We don't need to be on strike right now," \uc2 GM\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  said on Friday. Ahead of the strike last week, Ford Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  said: "We should be working creatively to solve hard problems rather than planning strikes and PR events."\par \par \uc2 The White House also got involved. President Biden said he plans to dispatch senior adviser Gene Sperling and acting Labor Secretary Julie Su to Detroit and urged the two sides to reach an agreement.\par \par \uc2 The strike so far has largely generated enthusiasm among the rank and file. "This was really nice because it kept everybody calm," said Ford Michigan Assembly worker Samantha Hart of Fain's targeted approach, as she walked the picket line early Friday. Hart's brother-in-law also walked out at the \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plant in Toledo.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  said Friday it will likely soon idle operations at its Chevrolet Malibu factory in Kansas City, Kan., because it would run short of parts that are shipped from the St. Louis-area plant that is shut down for the strike. Roughly 2,000 workers would be laid off if the plant is idled.\par \par \uc2 One of them is Rob Pacheco, who works the assembly line at the Malibu plant. He said it is unclear when or whether he will receive strike pay, because he isn't on strike. A \uc2 UAW\uc2  official said such situations will be decided case-by-case and that workers will be supported, whether through state unemployment or union funds.\par \par \uc2 Pacheco said his income uncertainty is worth the broader fight for a better deal. "I don't mind not being paid," he said. "We all knew what we were getting into."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Sean McLain contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230918ej9i00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
