\par\par \b \uc2 Whatever the Strike's Outcome, Musk Wins\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tim Higgins \par \uc2 577 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than two days into the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike against the Detroit automakers, one thing is clear: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has already won.\par \par \uc2 And the billionaire tycoon isn't even involved.\par \par \uc2 Musk won before the strike began early Friday. He won before negotiations started two months ago. From the get-go, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  were expected to spend more on wages because of the union's pressure. The question is just how much of an increase, and so far their offers haven't pleased the union, igniting this past week's work stoppage.\par \par \uc2 Whatever happens, more money surely will be spent. Any wage increase further advances \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's already tremendous cost advantage in EVs over its older U.S. peers, which are contending with generations of legacy expenses while trying to steer a costly transition to electric from gas-powered vehicles.\par \par \uc2 In March, Musk revealed plans to build on his advantage by setting a goal of slashing manufacturing costs for the automaker's next-generation vehicles by 50%, an ambitious agenda that will depend upon advanced automation, savvy engineering and other changes.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Tesla\uc2  chief executive's actions this year have shown how \uc2 Tesla\uc2  can take advantage of a lower cost structure to engage in price wars with rivals around the world to juice sales.\par \par \uc2 In July, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  reported second-quarter profit that rose 20% even after reducing prices. Around the same time, in the midst of steep losses from its electric vehicles, Ford said it would slow its EV production growth.\par \par \uc2 All this is playing out as the \uc2 UAW\uc2  seeks more than what the Detroit automakers say they can afford and remain competitive.\par \par \uc2 The Detroit companies' labor costs, including wages and benefits, are estimated at an average of $66 an hour, according to industry data. That compares with $45 at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which isn't unionized and was founded 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 A key difference between \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's compensation plan and that of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers revolves around company upside. \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers have been getting profit-sharing bonuses while \uc2 Tesla\uc2  workers receive stock options, which don't have a direct cash cost to the company. Over the years, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shares have risen like a rocket, though there have been periods of turbulence. Shares this year have more than doubled.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain argues that past contracts haven't kept up with inflation, hurting the union's roughly 146,000 auto workers' spending power, and that members' sacrifices have helped make the companies profitable in recent years."This is our generation's defining moment," Fain said late Thursday before the walkout. "The money is there. The cause is righteous."\par \par \uc2 Shortly afterward, Ford responded, saying the union's demands would "more than double Ford's current \uc2 UAW\uc2 -related labor costs, which are already significantly higher than the labor costs of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 Toyota\uc2  and other foreign-owned automakers in the United States that utilize non-union-represented labor."\par \par \uc2 Many analysts, so far, expect the Detroit companies will ultimately absorb the added costs. "The much larger issue is that it adds incremental pressure to their already challenged transition to an EV world," \uc2 Dan Levy\uc2 , an analyst for \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , cautioned investors in a recent note\par \par \uc2 Art Wheaton\uc2 , a labor expert at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 , suggests that the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's ability to get higher wages could help put heat on \uc2 Tesla\uc2  during any renewed effort to organize there.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230918ej9i0006c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
