\par\par \b \uc2 Barrymore Halts Recording Show Until Writers Strike Is Resolved\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ginger Adams Otis and Joe Flint \par \uc2 482 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Television host Drew Barrymore said Sunday she will delay the show's premiere after facing backlash for recording new episodes of her daytime show amid a monthslong writers' strike.\par \par \uc2 The show was set to return to the air Monday. Barrymore said she will now wait until the industry's labor issues with the Writers Guild of America are resolved.\par \par \uc2 "I have no words to express my deepest apologies to anyone I have hurt and, of course, to our incredible team who works on the show and has made it what it is today," Barrymore wrote on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . "We really tried to find our way forward. And I truly hope for a resolution for the entire industry very soon."\par \par \uc2 CBS Media Ventures, which produces "The Drew Barrymore Show," said in a statement Sunday that it supports Barrymore's decision.\par \par \uc2 "[We] understand how complex and difficult this process has been for her," the statement said.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  also said Sunday it will pause the season premiere of The Talk, which was due to air Monday. Another daytime talk show, The Jennifer Hudson Show, also opted to delay its Monday season premiere.\par \par \uc2 The reversals are a sign of the far-reaching impacts the writers' strike, along with one involving actors, are having across the entertainment industry, even in areas that possibly could resume work. It also highlights the power that striking actors, writers and their fans wield in an industry trying to weigh union solidarity against the need to restart projects so crew and other workers get paid.\par \par \uc2 Barrymore, who has three union writers on her show, resumed taping in New York last week. Striking workers picketed outside the studio in protest. The \uc2 National Book Foundation\uc2 , which presents the annual National Book Awards, rescinded its invitation to Barrymore to host the event.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Barrymore said that she owned the choice to go back to work despite the labor dispute and that she hoped it would bring people together.\par \par \uc2 The last season of her show finished taping in April, before the strike began. In May, she dropped out of hosting the \uc2 MTV\uc2  movie awards in an act of solidarity.\par \par \uc2 The WGA called for an industry strike in May, shutting down most writers rooms for movies and TV shows. Productions went dark, including daytime and late-night talk shows that depend on fast-thinking writers to make jokes out of the day's news.\par \par \uc2 Talk and variety shows operate under a different labor agreement than the one for movies and scripted television shows.\par \par \uc2 Other shows, such as ABC's, "The View," have continued during the strike and some are planning to return for the fall television season. There will be a shortage of guests for such shows as actors can't go on shows to promote films or television shows.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230918ej9i0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
