\par\par \b \uc2 Strike Hamstrings Auto Factories --- Walkouts at 3 plants ripple across others as Detroit carmakers announce layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert and Ryan Felton \par \uc2 1,125 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 The motor-vehicle-assembly data in a chart accompanying a Page One article Saturday about the United Auto Workers strike were seasonally adjusted at annual rates. The chart's note didn't say that the data were annualized.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ September 20, 2023)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike at three factories rippled across the industry Friday as Detroit automakers set plans to temporarily lay off workers at other plants hobbled by the union's unconventional tactics.\par \par \uc2 Some 12,700 workers went on strike across the three plants -- in Missouri, Ohio and Michigan -- in a strategy that represents a major test for \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain. Rather than strike against one company at a time with all of its union workers, he instead is taking on all three at once, but with targeted walkouts.\par \par \uc2 Already on Friday afternoon, not a full day into the strike, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  said it would soon idle a plant in Kansas because of a resulting parts shortage, putting about 2,000 employees out of work until production can resume. \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  said it would temporarily lay off about 600 workers following the walkout at its plant in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Fain is under pressure to get a deal done while not running down the union's strike fund, which pays picketing members. He also must meet auto workers' high expectations, which he has helped stoke since taking the top office.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the mood was mostly jubilant at the striking plants, punctuated by chants, loud music and blaring car horns. At one factory, "No deal, no wheels" became the rallying cry.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, negotiations with the automakers could continue; at the same time, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  could order more strikes.\par \par \uc2 The union began targeting plants at \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Jeep\uc2 -maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  that make some of the companies' more profitable and sought-after pickup trucks and SUVs, including the Jeep Wrangler and Ford Bronco.\par \par \uc2 The three plants collectively accounted for about 9% of the Detroit companies' overall vehicle production in North America, data from research firm Wards Intelligence shows.\par \par \uc2 Fain has contended the union is only getting started. The longer the negotiations continue, he has said, the more factories it plans to target, staging a series of sporadic walkouts to be executed with little notice and intended to scramble the car companies' production plans.\par \par \uc2 Members are also riled up, viewing it as their moment to make a statement.\par \par \uc2 "We got to make a sacrifice in order to get something," said Anthony Thompson, a factory worker at Ford's assembly plant in Wayne, Mich., which is among those on strike.\par \par \uc2 Thompson was sitting at home when the \uc2 UAW\uc2  called for a walkout at the plant shortly after midnight Friday. He immediately rushed to the picket line, wanting to tell his six grandchildren he was a part of history.\par \par \uc2 The strike is an unprecedented one, targeting all three Detroit automakers at once, and comes after nearly two months of bargaining failed to produce a deal at any one company.\par \par \uc2 With negotiators deadlocked, auto workers at three major assembly plants in Missouri, Ohio and Michigan walked off the job overnight, streaming out factory gates and collecting outside the entrances and crowding sidewalks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said he plans to dispatch White House senior adviser Gene Sperling and acting Labor Secretary Julie Su to Detroit in the coming days to offer support to the parties in reaching a fair contract.\par \par \uc2 Sperling and Su, who have been in daily touch with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and auto companies in recent weeks, won't be directly involved in the negotiations or playing the role of a mediator, according to people familiar with their plans.\par \par \uc2 Biden urged the two sides to reach an agreement and offered a message of solidarity with the workers. The president said Friday that while the auto companies have "made some significant offers," he believed that "they should go further to ensure record corporate profits mean record contracts for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Economists expect the strike's impact to be relatively modest if it is limited to a few locations and resolves quickly. A prolonged work stoppage could hold down economic growth, curtail employment and push up inflation, adding new uncertainty about the outlook for a cooling economy.\par \par \uc2 Until a new labor pact is reached, unionized employees at the companies' other factories will be working without contracts. The previous agreements, covering about 146,000 factory workers at \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , expired at 11:59 p.m. EDT Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Executives at the three automakers expressed frustration at the standoff, arguing they had each put forth lucrative bargaining proposals that would have secured wage increases of around 20% over four years for the unionized workers, along with hefty signing bonuses and one-time payments.\par \par \uc2 A Ford spokeswoman called the union's latest demands unsustainable and said the company was committed to reaching an agreement. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said it was disappointed in the strike, adding that union leaders refused to engage in a responsible manner.\par \par \uc2 "We didn't need to be here," \uc2 GM\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  told CNBC on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford were roughly flat Friday, while \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  closed up 2%.\par \par \uc2 Among the other factories now on strike are the \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  factory in Toledo, Ohio, where it makes the Jeep Wrangler, and \uc2 GM\uc2 's plant in Wentzville, Mo., where it produces the midsize Chevy Colorado and GMC Canyon.\par \par \uc2 At the end of August, all three companies had roughly a month or more of stock for the impacted models, based on the current selling pace. That could give them some cushion if the strike lingers.\par \par \uc2 The union, meanwhile, has built up a $825 million strike fund that it can stretch longer through more targeted strikes. Fain has sought to keep the companies on edge, and by striking all three at once, he is keeping up the pressure on each simultaneously, a tactic that he believes could lead to a better agreement sooner.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has pressed for some of its most ambitious demands in history, arguing that the car companies are coming off a historic run of profits and have doled out lucrative pay increases to their own CEOs.\par \par \uc2 It initially proposed a 40% pay bump for workers over four years and has sought the re-establishment cost-of-living adjustments and retiree medical benefits, both of which were lost in previous rounds of bargaining.\par \par \uc2 While the two sides have come closer together in pay, the carmakers say the union's demands would explode their labor costs, further widening a competitive gap with \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and foreign automakers.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ken Thomas and David Harrison contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich489\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7335
