\par\par \b \uc2 Auto Workers Launch Strike at Big Three --- Union walkout hits plants of competing carmakers in shift from previous tactics\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert and Mike Colias \par \uc2 916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  for the first time ever went on strike at all three Detroit car companies, with about 12,700 workers hitting the picket lines shortly after midnight in targeted work stoppages at plants in Michigan, Ohio and Missouri.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials initiated the walkout after failing to clinch new labor deals with \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  for about 146,000 U.S. factory workers. Bargaining went late into the night, but the two sides remained too far apart to avoid a walkout at the 11:59 p.m. Thursday deadline.\par \par \uc2 Workers at Ford's Bronco plant in Michigan, a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  Jeep factory in Toledo, Ohio, and a \uc2 GM\uc2  pickup plant in Missouri were instructed to leave their posts, beginning what could be a series of sporadic walkouts done without notice at additional auto factories.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain has described the approach as a way to sow confusion at the companies and give his negotiators more leverage at the bargaining table. He said more sites would likely go down if talks with the automakers stall.\par \par \uc2 "The money is there. The cause is righteous. The world is watching and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is ready to stand up," Fain told union members during a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  livestream. "This is our defining moment."\par \par \uc2 A Ford spokeswoman called the union's latest demands unsustainable, and said the company was committed to reaching an agreement. \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  didn't have comments.\par \par \uc2 Fain said the union has never conducted strikes across all three companies in the union's 88-year history. The \uc2 UAW\uc2 's plans for targeted work stoppages would bring only a fraction of the overall workforce off assembly lines.\par \par \uc2 That strategy would help preserve the union's $825 million fund more than a full strike of all 146,000 workers would, but stymie output and disrupt automakers' production planning. It also could prove risky, because employees who remain on the job likely would be working without a contract, a prospect that has sparked concern among some members.\par \par \uc2 As of Wednesday, the companies had offered wage increases of 17.5% to 20% over more than four years, short of the union's mid-30% demand, Fain said. Other issues also remain sticking points, such as cost-of-living adjustments and retiree medical benefits.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of a protracted strike is fraying nerves both within the auto industry and beyond Detroit.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has had calls with executives at the carmakers, as well as with the union chief. Investors are sizing up the potential hit to the bottom lines of the three automakers and their hundreds of publicly traded suppliers.\par \par \uc2 Economists are weighing the fallout from a potential labor disruption at \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which combined produce about half of the roughly 15 million vehicles made in the U.S. annually.\par \par \uc2 Fain, a firebrand who was elected by a slim majority of \uc2 UAW\uc2  members early this year, has framed the negotiations as a referendum on what the blue-collar workers deserve in an era where companies have been notching profit gains and other unions have scored major wage increases.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  said it put a new offer on the table Thursday morning with the goal of avoiding a strike. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said Wednesday that it was awaiting the union's response to its most recent offer and that it remained committed to reaching a tentative deal before the deadline.\par \par \uc2 The stalemate comes as both the union and the automakers attempt to reinvent themselves during widespread change in the car business.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders want to secure the union's place in the dawning electric-vehicle age, and assure that unionized workers fill the dozens of new battery and EV assembly plants that are springing up from tracts of farmland in Tennessee, Indiana and elsewhere. Fain also is vying to restore member trust after a multiyear bribery scandal rocked the union's leadership.\par \par \uc2 Automakers, meanwhile, are trying to transform themselves into lean, tech-forward companies.\par \par \uc2 The companies have funneled billions of dollars into EV and battery production, costly bets that could take years to pay off. They have to demonstrate to investors that they are reducing spending in other parts of the business, and a sharp increase in labor costs would come at a bad time.\par \par \uc2 Car executives also are wrestling with the high costs of moving to battery-powered vehicles as they race to close the gap on EV leader \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and worry that a richer labor agreement would further exacerbate their cost disadvantage to rivals, whose factory workforces aren't unionized.\par \par \uc2 Under the Detroit companies' current contract, hourly labor costs average out around $65, including benefits, compared with \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's $45 and $55 for Asian automakers.\par \par \uc2 Analysts estimate the union's initial demands would roughly double automakers' hourly labor costs. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has said that labor accounts for less than 5% of the companies' costs per vehicle.\par \par \uc2 A prolonged strike would have implications for consumers and the economy. The work stoppages would crimp the flow of cars to dealership lots and eventually reduce availability for car shoppers, just as inventory is being replenished after pandemic-era shortages.\par \par \uc2 Fain acknowledged on Wednesday that some members might be disappointed by the plan for limited walkouts. The targeted strike "provides us the maximum flexibility moving forward to have the most effective means of striking," he said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich355\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal5325
