\par\par \b \uc2 Strike Threat Clouds Auto Show in Detroit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Felton \par \uc2 688 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Detroit auto show has suffered setbacks in recent years, from pandemic disruptions to the defection of car brands. As the annual event kicked off Wednesday, it faced a new distraction: a looming strike deadline commanding the Motor City's attention.\par \par \uc2 Detroit's carmakers -- \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  -- are staring down a 11:59 p.m. ET Thursday deadline to reach tentative agreements with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  on new four-year labor contracts for roughly 146,000 factory workers. Industry observers anticipate a strike of at least one company starting early Friday, a day before the nine-day auto show opens to the public.\par \par \uc2 The gathering, officially named the North American International Auto Show, for years served as a cornerstone event on the industry's calendar. Dozens of the world's largest car brands would present large displays, unveil new models and outline their business goals for the year.\par \par \uc2 This year's show is considerably slimmed down, much like its return in September 2022 following a three-year hiatus because of Covid-19 precautions. Most major car brands -- including Asian automakers such as \uc2 Honda\uc2  and Hyundai, and German luxury names such as \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  and \uc2 BMW\uc2  -- are sitting out. Some have cited scheduling conflicts and a need to be more deliberative with how they spend marketing dollars.\par \par \uc2 The fading spectacle in Detroit mirrors a similar downsizing of marquee car shows globally. Even before the pandemic, participation among car companies was waning, as more brands sought their own spotlight through stand-alone events and social media. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in particular has leveraged such solo events, drawing thousands of enthusiastic fans and amplified media coverage.\par \par \uc2 Detroit's show this year has been more hollowed out than most, though. Just 15 automotive brands have displays, most of them owned by \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . That is down from 24 in 2019, and less than half of what the show carried a decade ago. Only six new models are scheduled to be revealed, compared with more than 40 at many past shows.\par \par \uc2 The Munich auto show last week featured dozens of global car brands, including many Chinese automakers. Annual shows in New York and Los Angeles also have attracted better car-company participation than Detroit in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The Detroit show "really was that place where the latest and greatest would be presented, and it would be such a spectacle," said Jessica Caldwell, head of insights at car-shopping site Edmunds. "It is very different now."\par \par \uc2 Rod Alberts, executive director at the Detroit show, said the landscape for auto shows has changed, particularly as automakers have opted to reveal more models through livestreams and other avenues.\par \par \uc2 "As a show, we need to evolve, as well," Alberts said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Detroit, auto executives and \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials have been negotiating for about two months, but appear far apart on the union's demand for a considerable wage hike. Unlike prior behind-closed-doors talks, new \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain has been publicly disclosing details of the negotiations, generating news coverage and stoking speculation that the union might strike all three automakers.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  members have used the auto show as a public stage to air their demands in the past. In 2019, workers staged a candlelight vigil outside the auto show's charity event to protest the automaker's proposed effort to idle five North American factories.\par \par \uc2 Similar to 2022, this year's Detroit show emphasizes hands-on experiences, such as test drives, ride-alongs and interactive displays. It also includes some nonautomotive displays, including air taxis, or eVTOLs, short for electric vertical takeoff and landing.\par \par \uc2 A two-day forum is scheduled concurrently featuring speakers focusing on technology and mobility, including author Malcolm Gladwell.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, a day reserved for media previews, there were some of the familiar auto-show trappings, with glitzy displays and a few onstage reveals of new models set to booming music. \uc2 Jeep\uc2  showed a new vehicle, while Ford debuted a refreshed F-150 pickup truck this week at an event near the show.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230914ej9e00065\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
