\par\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2  Plans Targeted Strikes on Automakers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Felton and Nora Eckert \par \uc2 735 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  plans to hold targeted strikes at certain U.S. auto factories if it can't reach new labor deals with the Detroit automakers by late Thursday, an unusual strategy that could broadly disrupt assembly-line production.\par \par \uc2 In a video call with union local leaders Tuesday, \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain rolled out the work-stoppage plans, which also call for the actions to escalate the longer the union goes without a new contract agreement, including picketing more plants, according to people familiar with the presentation.\par \par \uc2 If an agreement isn't looking likely, \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials intend to announce which factories will go on strike late Thursday, within hours of when the union's current contracts with the car companies are set to expire, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Fain is calling the walkout a "stand up" strike, a play on the 1930s sit-down strike at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  in Flint, Mich.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 UAW\uc2  spokesman said the union isn't commenting on strategy. Fain is expected to outline the strike plans during remarks on a livestream Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is negotiating new four-year labor agreements with \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  for roughly 146,000 hourly workers at the companies' U.S. factories.\par \par \uc2 Fain, on the video call, expressed doubt about reaching an agreement with any of the companies before the strike deadline.\par \par \uc2 The Detroit Free Press earlier reported the strike plans Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The union is pressing for a double-digit pay increase, more paid time off and the return of cost-of-living adjustments. The automakers want to keep their labor costs in check and gain more flexibility in how they staff their plants, including continued use of temporary workers.\par \par \uc2 The sides have frequently exchanged offers in recent days, and there has been slight progress on discussions around pay.\par \par \uc2 Historically, targeted strikes have focused on engine and transmission plants, which make parts for a broader number of assembly factories. Fain hasn't yet shared what sorts of facilities this strike will focus on.\par \par \uc2 A targeted walkout would allow the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to limit the use of its strike fund by sending fewer workers to the picket lines and apply more pressure to the automakers.\par \par \uc2 A handful of plants operated by the companies employ approximately 12,000 workers but supply most North American transmissions used in vehicles, according to a July note from \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  analysts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has a $825 million strike fund to offset members' wages lost during a strike. The union provides workers who have walked out $500 per week.\par \par \uc2 There is precedent for this type of strike strategy within the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . The targeted strike approach was used in the 1930s and '40s, and notably at a contentious walkout in 1998 at \uc2 GM\uc2 , said Alex Colvin, dean of \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 's industrial relations school.\par \par \uc2 "It does have a long history, even though in more recent decades it has been less common in auto bargaining than these broader strikes," Colvin said.\par \par \uc2 The 1998 strike at plants in Flint, Mich. led to a cascade of additional closures of other \uc2 GM\uc2  factories that depended on their parts.\par \par \uc2 The 54-day action cost the automaker $2.2 billion, and economists estimated that it shaved about half a percentage point off gross-domestic-product growth in the second quarter of that year.\par \par \uc2 Fain, elected this spring to lead the 88-year-old union, has repeatedly taken an unorthodox approach to negotiations.\par \par \uc2 He kicked off talks by doing away with the traditional handshake with auto executives, has held frequent livestreams updating members on negotiations and made the details of company proposals public.\par \par \uc2 Automakers have built up inventory ahead of a potential strike, which would give them some cushion to keep selling cars during a work-stoppage before they run out.\par \par \uc2 At the end of August, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  -- whose brands also include Ram, Chrysler, and Dodge -- had 74 days of unsold inventory, a measure of how much stock is available if the company's plants were immediately shut down, according to data from Motor Intelligence, an industry-research firm.\par \par \uc2 The automaker this summer had been running certain plants that make some Jeep models at "critical status," allowing them to run up to seven days a week.\par \par \uc2 By comparison, Ford had 64 days of supply, while \uc2 GM\uc2  had 50 days. The industry's average was 38 days.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230913ej9d0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
