\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: California Healthcare Workers' Pay to Rise Under Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christine Mai-Duc \par \uc2 456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Labor and industry groups in California have reached the second deal in two days that would raise wage floors for workers and bring a detente to bitter compensation fights that have become common in specific sectors in recent years.\par \par \uc2 A last-minute agreement announced by healthcare facilities and SEIU California late Monday would lift minimum pay for healthcare workers in the state to $25 an hour over several years. The pay increases could affect nearly half a million healthcare workers statewide, according to June estimates from the UC Berkeley Labor Center.\par \par \uc2 In return, state law would pre-empt unions and local officials from increasing healthcare workers' pay via local ballot measures for 10 years. Local minimum-wage increases would still be allowed under the deal, but would have to apply to all sectors.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, fast-food industry groups and labor leaders unveiled a similar deal that set minimum wages for their workers at $20 an hour starting in April. The measures are among hundreds legislators are considering during the final days of their session, and will require signatures from Democratic Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Amendments to an existing bill related to healthcare workers, which would have mandated a $25-an-hour minimum wage starting in 2025, were published late Monday.\par \par \uc2 The timeline for healthcare-worker pay increases would vary depending on where the employees work.\par \par \uc2 Workers employed at dialysis clinics and healthcare systems with 10,000 or more employees, for example, would earn $23 an hour starting next year, $24 an hour in 2025 and $25 an hour in 2026.Hospitals in rural regions or places with high numbers of Medicare patients, however, would be required to pay only $18 an hour in 2024, rising to $25 by 2033. California's minimum wage is currently set at $15.50 an hour and will rise to $16 an hour on Jan. 1.\par \par \uc2 According to an analysis by the UC Berkeley Labor Center, a $25-an-hour wage for healthcare workers would represent an average 31% increase for those workers statewide.\par \par \uc2 A separate deal crafted by unions and dialysis clinics statewide promises that neither side would seek to change wages, working conditions or other rules for those facilities using state legislation or ballot measures for four years.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Newsom, who endorsed the fast-food deal on Monday, didn't immediately respond to a request for comment on the healthcare-industry compromise.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic and the high cost of living in California have led to worker burnout and healthcare-worker shortages. A 2021 study by Mercer estimated that California would experience a shortage of 500,000 low-wage healthcare workers by 2026.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230913ej9d0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
