\par\par \b \uc2 Unions Flex New Clout to Get Hefty Raises in Key Industries\b0\par\par\uc2 By Austen Hufford \par \uc2 790 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Labor unions are exerting clout not seen in decades, winning big pay increases and better benefits in industries beset with worker shortages.\par \par \uc2 Airlines, package shippers and ports all struggled to hire as the economy emerged from the Covid-19 pandemic and then agreed to double-digit raises with their unions this summer.\par \par \uc2 Whether other employers without such hiring difficulties -- including car manufacturers facing a strike threat from the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  -- follow suit remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 Pilots at \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  ratified a contract last month boosting wages by more than 40% over its four-year term. West Coast dockworkers secured a 32% raise through 2028 this summer. \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  workers affiliated with the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  struck a deal that will increase top hourly pay by 18% over the five years. The agreement will push the average full-time driver to $170,000 annually in pay and benefits, the \uc2 UPS\uc2  chief executive said.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, the unions' latest gains just catch up to pay increases that nonunion workers landed since the pandemic hit the U.S. economy in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Wages and benefits for nonunion workers were up 15.8% from the end of 2019 through June, compared with a 12.2% gain for unionized workers, according to the Labor Department. Union workers earn more on average but accounted for just about 10% of the workforce in 2022, a record-low share.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  made a recent proposal asking for a mid-30% raise over four years, rather than the 40% increase the union initially targeted. Aside from pay, the union has also called for a shortened 32-hour week, the re-establishment of medical benefits for retirees, and the return of cost-of-living adjustments.\par \par \uc2 In transportation, warehousing and utilities combined, the job-openings rate -- a reflection of labor demand -- exceeded the average for private-sector employers in July. The group employs pilots, dockworkers and truckers.\par \par \uc2 For manufacturers of long-lasting durable goods, including autos, the rate is below average and falling this year.\par \par \uc2 "Why are the pilots doing well? Why are the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  doing well? Both had a lot of bargaining leverage," said Harry Katz, a professor who studies collective bargaining at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 , referring to the limited numbers of workers relative to demand in those fields.\par \par \uc2 Hiring of transportation and warehousing workers surged in recent years when the pandemic accelerated consumers' shift to online purchases, resulting in often daily visits from truck drivers, including those at \uc2 UPS\uc2 . Employment in these industries is up 16% since the end of 2019, versus 3% overall.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, total U.S. manufacturing employment is up less than 2% since the end of 2019 and down a third from its late 1970s peak, reflecting increased automation and foreign competition.\par \par \uc2 The airline industry still grapples with worker shortages. Travel initially stalled during the pandemic, causing pilots to retire and training programs to halt. Then eager travelers emerged from lockdowns and hiring new pilots took time because it takes years of training to fly an airliner.\par \par \uc2 "Because of supply and demand of pilots, they had no choice" but to agree to large pay increases, \uc2 Dennis Tajer\uc2 , a pilot at \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and a spokesman for the \uc2 Allied Pilots Association\uc2 , said of company management.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  members employed by \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  authorized their leaders to call a strike after an existing contract expires Thursday. Those three companies produce about half of the vehicles made in the U.S., according to the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, the immediate disruption to consumers from a \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike would likely be more limited than a major airline shutting down or closed ports blocking goods from entering the U.S. Shoppers would still be able to buy new vehicles already on dealer lots and those made by manufacturers such as \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  that don't have unionized U.S. factory workers.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  members previously gave concessions when the industry was weaker, including when \uc2 GM\uc2  and Chrysler, a predecessor to \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , filed for bankruptcy in 2009. The top hourly wage for \uc2 UAW\uc2  production workers didn't increase between 2006 and 2015, the \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The latest talks come as automakers shift toward making more electric vehicles, which put jobs at traditional engine and transmission plants at risk.\par \par \uc2 Two notable unions in an industry without as severe labor shortages have been unable to extract large pay gains so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood writers have been striking since this spring and were joined in July by actors.\par \par \uc2 These union members could have less leverage than pilots and truckers because they work in entertainment industries where job seekers are plentiful.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich297\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4455
