\par\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Eases Office Return For Senior Executives --- CEO Calhoun travels on company jets from two homes, while CFO West has a workplace five minutes from his doorstep\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Tangel and Mark Maremont \par \uc2 2,079 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's senior executives, returning to the office has been a smooth flight.\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive \uc2 David Calhoun\uc2 , who took over the company just before the pandemic, started working from home and then never made the move when \uc2 Boeing\uc2  shifted its headquarters from Chicago to Arlington, Va., last year.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Calhoun travels via private jet from two homes, one a sprawling waterfront house at New Hampshire's Lake Sunapee, the other in a gated South Carolina resort community. \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's fleet of private jets have made more than 400 trips to or from airports near Calhoun's homes in the past three years, according to flight records reviewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Chief Financial Officer \uc2 Brian West\uc2 , who joined \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in August 2021, hasn't relocated from his home in New Canaan, Conn. The company recently opened a small office about five minutes from his house.\par \par \uc2 When a reporter entered the New Canaan office through a propped-open door for an unannounced visit on a midsummer Monday morning, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's second-highest-ranking executive was wearing a polo shirt, shorts and slip-on shoes. West showed a spartan office that he uses, but otherwise declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , like companies all over the U.S., is trying to navigate a complicated postpandemic workplace environment. Although some positions require full-time attendance, the company is allowing hybrid work for many jobs. About 30% of recent \uc2 Boeing\uc2  job postings were for hybrid or remote positions.\par \par \uc2 Managers eager to get employees back to the Arlington office over the past two years have turned to happy hours, guest speakers and even visiting alpacas, say people who have worked there. Calhoun and West are seldom spotted in the building, they say.\par \par \uc2 "What's he doing? Is he like at Lake Sunapee or something in New Hampshire?" CNBC host \uc2 Jim Cramer\uc2  said on TV about Calhoun in 2021, as the company dealt with mounting factory problems with its 787 Dreamliner, a wide-body jet. "I mean, what is he doing?"\par \par \uc2 After the Cramer episode, some \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employees in Arlington made light of the CEO's absence, with about a half dozen of them displaying wooden "Lake Sunapee" signs in their offices, according to people familiar with the matter. There were also Lake Sunapee souvenir mugs, including one that read: "Love Lake Life."\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said Calhoun visited the Dreamliner factory during its problems. He said top executives, while they enjoy more perquisites than lower-ranking personnel, are using the same flexibility afforded to many other white-collar employees. Relocating C-suite executives and their families to Arlington would be costly, he said, and their jobs require them to travel frequently anyway.\par \par \uc2 "Rather than this ivory tower corporate headquarters approach, there's another approach, which is: Encourage leaders to travel, get out there and engage the best they can and not worry about sitting tied to a desk at a traditional corporate HQ," the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Calhoun is required by \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's board to fly on company-supplied private jets for all business and personal trips for security reasons.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said its New Canaan office, which opened this spring, was necessary to recruit the company's new treasurer, David Whitehouse. (Property records show Whitehouse lives about 30 minutes away.) The New Canaan space saved relocation expenses for moving Whitehouse, who oversees treasury staff mostly based in Chicago, the company said. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  also said the office would afford easy access to investment bankers and ratings firms in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's space leased for more than $100,000 a year, according to \uc2 CoStar\uc2 , an outside real-estate data firm.\par \par \uc2 It's unusual but not unheard of for a CEO to live and work remotely far from the home office. Still, Peter Cappelli, a management professor at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania's Wharton School\uc2  and author of a recent book about remote work, "The Future of the Office," said it's out of step with the general messaging from corporate America that encourages employees to return to the office.\par \par \uc2 "If you want people to come back and you're not doing it, that really undermines the message," Cappelli said.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  appears to be an outlier among its peers. At rival \uc2 Airbus\uc2 , CEO \uc2 Guillaume Faury\uc2  regularly works from the European plane maker's headquarters in Toulouse, France, when he isn't traveling for work, a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Small-plane and helicopter maker \uc2 Textron\uc2  requires all its white-collar employees to be at the office full-time, and CEO \uc2 Scott Donnelly\uc2  works from the company's Providence, R.I., headquarters, a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  said CEO \uc2 Jim Taiclet\uc2  spends about half his time working from the company's headquarters in Bethesda, Md., and has a nearby residence but uses company aircraft to travel to another home.\par \par \uc2 Calhoun, a former \uc2 General Electric\uc2  executive and longtime \uc2 Boeing\uc2  director, became \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's CEO in early 2020. The board appointed him to succeed \uc2 Dennis Muilenburg\uc2  and help the company navigate the fallout from two crashes of its 737 MAX in 2018 and 2019, which took 346 lives. Shortly after he became CEO, Calhoun bought a condo in Chicago near the company's then-headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Calhoun and West have focused on improving \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's operations and returning the company to financial strength. They have moved to cut costs, including by trimming payrolls and office space. \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's stock has lost about 35% of its value since Calhoun took over as CEO, but the company last year generated free cash flow, a closely watched earnings metric, for the first time since 2018. It has resumed delivering its MAX and Dreamliner jets and increased production after working through engineering and regulatory snags.\par \par \uc2 "There has been undeniable progress over the last three years," the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said. The company's second-quarter earnings, a $149 million loss due largely to defense-unit charges, beat analyst expectations and the stock jumped 9%.\par \par \uc2 Calhoun began running the company from home when the Covid-19 pandemic forced much of the country to avoid the workplace. Even after the pandemic, Calhoun has said he wouldn't be traveling much to visit his staff. "Video meetings are amazingly effective for the personal efficiency of a CEO," he told the writers of the 2021 book "Leading at a Distance."\par \par \uc2 Speaking after a June 2022 press event in Arlington at which \uc2 Boeing\uc2  formally introduced its new global head office, Calhoun described remote work as part of his job. "Remember now what headquarters is -- it's me, CFO," he said, referring to himself and West.\par \par \uc2 "Seventy percent of my day, no matter where I am, is virtual anyway, because I run a large distributed company," he said.\par \par \uc2 The morning of that event, one of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's corporate jets took off from a small airport near Calhoun's New Hampshire home, landing 68 minutes later at Dulles International Airport near the company's headquarters. After the announcement, the jet flew from Dulles to Seattle -- \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commercial hub -- then back to New Hampshire the following day, flight records show.\par \par \uc2 Without access to passenger manifests, the Journal couldn't determine who was on board any particular \uc2 Boeing\uc2  private-jet flight.\par \par \uc2 The flight patterns suggest Calhoun travels directly from his New Hampshire or South Carolina homes to the Arlington headquarters an average of once or twice a month. He doesn't have a residence in the Washington, D.C., area, the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Some flight records coincide with Calhoun's known whereabouts. One of the company's Bombardier Challenger 650s flew from the Hilton Head, S.C., airport to Dulles on Jan. 31, 2022 before setting off for a Monterey, Calif. airfield close to an \uc2 AT&T\uc2  Pebble Beach Pro-Am golf event. Calhoun was scheduled to play alongside former Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice and two professional golfers. A tournament spokesman confirmed that Calhoun did play.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian-made jet stayed near Pebble Beach for a week before flying to Seattle, Nashville and then to Lebanon, N.H., near Calhoun's lake home.\par \par \uc2 Calhoun was at the Arlington headquarters on Tuesday, May 9, this year to announce a major deal with European discount airline \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  to buy up to 300 of its 737 MAX jets. Flight records show a \uc2 Boeing\uc2 -owned Challenger flying the prior day to Dulles from the Savannah/Hilton Head airport, then taking off for Lebanon, N.H., shortly after the \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  announcement.\par \par \uc2 Calhoun's hands-off approach to management has at times made him seem less visible than some prior \uc2 Boeing\uc2  leaders, who spent more time at headquarters and held more frequent meetings, current and former executives say.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said Calhoun holds meetings for all employees, all vice presidents and his senior executive council each quarter. The senior-executive meetings are held at various locations, where the leaders spend time with employees at their worksites. "\uc2 Dave Calhoun\uc2  is very engaged with our global workforce and outside stakeholders," the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 One of the largest groups at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Arlington site is its government-relations staff, many of whom are back at the office most of the time when not meeting officials, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  defense executives based in Arlington primarily work from the office when they're not traveling, the company spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 In the Seattle area, unionized \uc2 Boeing\uc2  technicians who work with factory machinists are generally required to show up to work full-time, while engineers tend to have more flexibility with office attendance, union officials said.\par \par \uc2 Although the union applauds \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's flexible approach overall, some employees occasionally bristle at being required to do certain tasks in person when the work could be done remotely, said \uc2 Rich Plunkett\uc2 , a union official for the \uc2 Society of Professional Engineering Employees in Aerospace\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "People are pissed they're being told to get their butts to the office," he said.\par \par \uc2 The company plans to shrink its presence at the former Chicago headquarters, where it occupies 11 floors in the 36-story skyscraper, the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Two senior \uc2 Boeing\uc2  executives live in the Orlando area. One, communications chief Brian Besanceney, hired in 2022, has his official office in Arlington and has worked from home when he's not traveling, the spokesman said. The other, human-resources chief \uc2 Michael D'Ambrose\uc2 , joined \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in mid-2020 and hasn't relocated to Arlington.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said D'Ambrose's primary worksite is at a company facility near Orlando, which it recently outfitted for him at minimal cost -- around $1,300 for new keys, webcams and a printer.\par \par \uc2 Having a primary work location at a satellite office close to home could save executives money on their personal taxes, according to tax specialists. That's because private jet flights taken for commuting purposes -- for example, from an executive's home to headquarters -- generally are a taxable perquisite, while flights between two company offices may not be.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's unit that manages executive flight operations closely tracks whether flights on company aircraft are for personal or business trips, say people familiar with \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's internal practices. The company, including its board of directors, monitors personal aircraft usage, which is a perk disclosed to investors.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman declined to address executives' personal taxes but said office locations weren't chosen for tax reasons. He said the company has a rigorous process to ensure personal aircraft use complies with laws and regulations.\par \par \uc2 Calhoun's reported $22.5 million in compensation in 2022 included $238,782 for personal use of company aircraft, according to company filings. The prior year his disclosed personal aircraft usage totaled $83,563, plus $50,026 in relocation costs that included aircraft expenses. The \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said Calhoun's travel increased as the pandemic faded.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said Calhoun has sought to reduce executives' use of company aircraft.\par \par \uc2 West, the chief financial officer, joined the company in August 2021. The \uc2 Boeing\uc2  spokesman said West, D'Ambrose and Besanceney also take commercial flights.\par \par \uc2 Inside the New Canaan office, a model jetliner sat on a coffee table in a reception area. Outside there was no \uc2 Boeing\uc2  sign advertising the tenant, which shares the building with the local school district's new offices.\par \par \uc2 A mailbox label inside a vestibule read "TBC," internal code for \uc2 The Boeing Company\uc2 .\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw304\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4560\pichgoal9600
