\par\par \b \uc2 Carmakers, \uc2 UAW\uc2  in Profit Tug of War\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Colias and Nora Eckert \par \uc2 814 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Detroit automakers are riding a multiyear run of soaring profits. How that cash pile should be deployed is at the heart of a looming showdown with the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2 , which is threatening to strike Friday.\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  are negotiating new four-year labor contracts with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  for about 146,000 U.S. factory workers.\par \par \uc2 The car companies say they need the money to fund their switch to electric vehicles; the union says its workers deserve more cash because of high inflation, their work during the pandemic and concessions made in past negotiations to help the automakers survive darker times.\par \par \uc2 Weeks of intense talks and combative public volleys have yet to produce a deal for any of the automakers. Union leaders say members are prepared to hit picket lines at any company that hasn't reached a tentative labor agreement after the current contracts expire at 11:59 p.m. Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The talks in Detroit are exposing an uncomfortable tension between the automakers' recent profit streak and car executives' warnings of a steep cost curve ahead as they race to produce more EVs. Automakers' early EV models generate smaller profits than their internal-combustion counterparts and often are money losers.\par \par \uc2 Auto executives worry that absorbing a big labor-cost increase would put them on unequal footing with rivals, especially as they spend money to build out their electric offerings and close the gap with EV leader \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the short run, a factory walkout would threaten to disrupt Detroit's rollout of several new EV models and potentially drain billions of dollars in profits. It could also further narrow selection on dealership lots. Vehicle inventories are finally rebounding after years of pandemic-related shortages, which left many buyers frustrated and paying higher prices for new cars.\par \par \uc2 The industry's last major U.S. strike was in 2019, when 46,000 workers walked out of \uc2 GM\uc2 's factories for 40 days. The work stoppage erased about $3.6 billion from the company's bottom line.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders see this round of negotiations as a prime chance to recoup significant concessions made over the past two decades, especially around the 2007-09 financial crisis. The union gave up lucrative job-security guarantees and retiree benefits as the automakers sustained heavy losses, with both \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 -predecessor Chrysler eventually entering bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 Unions representing workers in other industries -- including at \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  -- have landed sizable wage increases recently, adding momentum for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's ambitious demands. U.S. workers have also gained leverage during the tight labor market of the past few years, and a period of record inflation has left many demanding and receiving higher pay.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders and rank-and-file workers say the costs involved in the EV transition aren't an excuse to skimp on worker raises.\par \par \uc2 "It's not just about money. It's so much more than this," said Elwanza Guess, a worker at a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  plant in Michigan, during a practice strike last month. "The companies, they have taken, taken, taken, but not appreciated the workers that are helping them get there."\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain has repeated his mantra "record profits mean record contracts," and points to inflationary pressures that have stung workers' pocketbooks at a time when auto CEOs have been granted raises.\par \par \uc2 Typically by this point in the negotiation process, union leaders would be focused on reaching a deal with one company. But \uc2 UAW\uc2  negotiators continue to bargain with all three, rather than targeting one, raising the possibility that the union could strike more than one of the companies if they can't reach deals by the late-Thursday deadlines.\par \par \uc2 A strike at all three companies would send union workers at dozens of U.S. factories to the picket lines, a work stoppage without much precedent in automotive history.\par \par \uc2 Even a 10-day walkout on this scale could lead to $5 billion in lost economic activity for the auto industry, according to an analysis by Michigan-based Anderson Economic Group.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has an $825 million strike fund to help offset members' lost wages during a strike. Thatamount is enough to cover a roughly two-month walkout at all three automakers, analysts estimate.\par \par \uc2 The union could also take a more targeted approach, striking at key transmission or engine plants that would cut off supplies of components to other assembly plants. It could also choose to strike just one company, as it did in the last round of talks.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders are pressing for at least a 40% wage increase across the four years of the contracts. They are also demanding40 hours of pay for a 32-hour workweek, restoration of retiree medical benefits and a crackdown on the use of temporary workers.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich468\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7020
