\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- The Score / The Business Week in 6 Stocks: \uc2 Apple\uc2  Slips on China Ban, SEC Probes \uc2 GameStop\uc2  Chair\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francesca Fontana \par \uc2 593 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\par \par \uc2 UAL -2.5%\par \par \uc2 Airlines are warning that higher fuel costs are eating into profits. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  analysts on Tuesday cut price targets across the industry, citing the high prices. Jet fuel prices have increased 24% since the start of the industry's second-quarter earnings season. \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  said Wednesday it expects third-quarter fuel costs between $2.95 and $3.05 a gallon. Back in July, the airline's third-quarter guidance estimated a per-gallon range of $2.50 and $2.80. \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  also said fuel prices would be higher than previously forecast. United shares fell 2.5% Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 WARNER BROS. DISCOVERY\par \par \uc2 WBD\uc2  -1.3%\par \par \uc2 The ongoing Hollywood labor strikes are weighing on \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2 's bottom line. The entertainment conglomerate cut its full-year profit outlook by $500 million in a securities filing Tuesday and said it expects the fallout to affect the company through the end of 2023 \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 . said the strikes are lasting longer than expected, and that it can't predict when they will end. Chief Executive \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2  on Wednesday participated in an investor conference, during which he discussed the guidance revision. \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 . shares dropped 1.3% Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 GAMESTOP\par \par \uc2 GME -6.2%\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  is investigating billionaire \uc2 Ryan Cohen\uc2 's ownership -- and surprise sale -- of \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  shares. The \uc2 GameStop\uc2  executive chairman took a $120 million stake in the struggling housewares retailer, but abruptly sold in August 2022, netting a nearly $60 million profit. Cohen's interest spurred a frenzy of trading that caused its stock to soar 34% in a day before collapsing when he disclosed the sales. \uc2 GameStop\uc2  shares slid 6.2% Friday.\par \par \uc2 APPLE\par \par \uc2 AAPL -3.6%\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  is facing new threats to its iPhone sales in China. The Wall Street Journal on Wednesday reported China has ordered government officials not to use iPhones for work or bring them to the office. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported Thursday that such a ban might also be extended to state-owned enterprises and other government-backed entities. The directive is part of Beijing's campaign to cut reliance on foreign technology and could threaten \uc2 Apple\uc2 's China business. \uc2 Apple\uc2 's stock slumped, costing the company about $194 billion in market value. Its shares lost 3.6% Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  -0.8%\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and other automakers are still butting heads with the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  over pay and other benefits -- one week out from a strike deadline. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  called \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ' Thursday counteroffer, including a 10% proposed wage increase -- far short of the union's 40% ask -- insulting. The offer follows a similar recent proposal from \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , which called for a 9% wage increase. \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford, and \uc2 Jeep\uc2 -maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have until Sept. 14 to reach tentative agreements. \uc2 GM\uc2  shares ended 0.8% lower Thursday.\par \par \uc2 KROGER\par \par \uc2 KR +3.1%\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2 's stock rallied after a spate of announcements on Friday. The grocery giant swung to a second-quarter loss as it incurred a $1.4 billion charge to resolve opioid-related claims. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  said the companies would sell more than 400 stores to \uc2 C&S Wholesale Grocers\uc2  to complete their proposed merger. C&S, which operates Grand Union groceries and the Piggly Wiggly franchise, will pay \uc2 Kroger\uc2  about $1.9 billion in cash. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  said the merger is on track to close in early 2024. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  shares rose 3.1% Friday.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich349\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5235
