\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 GM\uc2 's Latest Counteroffer to \uc2 UAW\uc2  Shows Gap Remains\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 484 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  delivered a counteroffer to the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  Thursday that shows the sides remain far apart on pay and other benefits, one week out from a strike deadline.\par \par \uc2 The Detroit carmaker offered a 10% wage increase among other benefits, far short of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's demand of at least a 40% wage increase. The offer follows a similar one from \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  last week, which called for a 9% wage increase. Both companies proposed additional inflation-protection payments and signing bonuses totaling more than $16,000.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain in a statement called the \uc2 GM\uc2  proposal insulting and said the company is wasting union members' time. Last week, Fain responded similarly to the proposal from Ford.\par \par \uc2 Jeep\uc2 -maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  is planning to deliver its counteroffer to the union by the end of the week, a spokeswoman said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The three companies face a deadline of Sept. 14 at 11:59 p.m. to reach tentative agreements with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  on a new four-year contract. Fain has said the union is prepared to strike at any of the three companies that haven't reached a deal by that date, potentially taking 146,000 factory workers off the job.\par \par \uc2 The negotiations are playing out in a much more public way than during past talks. Fain, who was elected earlier this year, has broadcast specifics of company proposals directly to members in weekly livestreams, and the automakers have posted details to their websites. The back-and-forth is a departure from traditional behind-closed-doors bargaining practices.\par \par \uc2 Since becoming \uc2 UAW\uc2  president in March, Fain has done away with much of the traditional \uc2 UAW\uc2  bargaining playbook. To formally kick off contract talks, he bypassed the ceremonial handshake with company CEOs and instead met with workers.\par \par \uc2 Fain also has opted to not select any one company as the union's negotiating target, doing away with the traditional \uc2 UAW\uc2  strategy of hammering out a deal with one company to serve as a template for the other two. That approach makes a walkout at all three automakers a possibility once contracts expire next week.\par \par \uc2 Some auto executives have called Fain's actions theatrical, especially when he tossed an early proposal from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  into a trash can during a livestream.\par \par \uc2 The union has rallied members through practice picket events around factories in different states, many of which Fain has attended. Those gatherings are slated to continue over the coming week.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  filed complaints last week with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  citing unfair labor practices by \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , saying the two companies were bargaining in bad faith and delaying negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Both companies said they were surprised by the charges and denied the allegations. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  called the complaint frivolous and a distraction, and \uc2 GM\uc2  said it was an insult to the bargaining committees.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230908ej980000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
