\par\par \b \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  Struggles to Revamp\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Eisen \par \uc2 950 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  is still trying to fix itself.\par \par \uc2 Seven years after a fake-accounts scandal engulfed the bank, thousands of employees are grappling with how to best catch or prevent problems in everything it does.\par \par \uc2 Each day, employees gather in virtual and in-person "workshops" to map out the risks that come with big moves such as foreclosing on a home and small moves like allowing a customer-service representative to override a late fee. How best to manage those risks is debated. Employees regularly present before a panel of superiors, and their work either is waved through or sent back.\par \par \uc2 Risk exercises are common at many companies, but the stakes are higher at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . The bank needs to upgrade its risk and control functions to its own liking -- and to satisfy regulators.\par \par \uc2 The Office of the Comptroller of the Currency\uc2 , \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 's primary regulator, raised the possibility in January of breaking up banks that can't put adequate controls in place. It didn't mention \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  specifically, but the bank was widely seen as a focus of the remarks.\par \par \uc2 The bank's stock is down about 15% since the scandal erupted in September 2016, while \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 's has more than doubled. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  hasn't been able to shake a 2018 order from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that handcuffed its ability to grow. And the bank, once a prolific buyer of other banks, has been cutting staff and shrinking business lines.\par \par \uc2 For many inside \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 , it feels like a make-or-break moment.\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive Officer \uc2 Charlie Scharf\uc2  spoke at a conference in May about what he perceived to be regulators' growing willingness to take drastic action when banks aren't meeting their expectations. "Until our work is done, we're still at risk," he said.\par \par \uc2 The risk exercises are expected to last for at least another year, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 In these workshops, technically called "risk control self assessment," employees document how a banking service works, the risks, the controls that mitigate those risks and any gaps that need to be filled with new controls. In a bank, controls do such things as alerting a teller to ask for identification before cashing a check.\par \par \uc2 The work sometimes has setbacks. Last year, the control work for check and cash processing at the bank's back-end operations centers returned to the drawing board because there was no control in place for employee fraud, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Banking today looks less like a collection of corner branches than webs of digital systems that move money around the globe. Many banks rely on aging technology accumulated through decades of acquisitions. The challenge is to rework these complex webs while juggling heavy regulatory scrutiny and avoiding business disruptions.\par \par \uc2 "It takes a long time to turn a big ship," said \uc2 Jason Goldberg\uc2 , an analyst at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . "And \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  is certainly one of the biggest."\par \par \uc2 The task is all the more challenging for \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 , which, once the scandal broke, was criticized for having a culture that was insular and decentralized.\par \par \uc2 "If you can change a culture in seven years, you've done a good job," said \uc2 Thomas Hoenig\uc2 , former president of the \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2  and former vice chairman of the \uc2 Federal Deposit Insurance Corp\uc2 ., who hasn't directly supervised \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . "If you can't have major progress after seven years, you're probably not doing it right."\par \par \uc2 Scharf said in a letter to shareholders earlier this year that the bank needs to meet regulators' expectations for risk and controls with the speed and quality they expect. "This will remain our top priority," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The urgency to right the ship and the strain that effort entails, is felt across the bank's workforce, current and former employees said. A manager wrote in January to his team that works on internal controls, "Each of us is under tremendous stress, whether we acknowledge it or not, and that stress builds over time."\par \par \uc2 Some workers doing the risk exercises have had to tell their families that they might be scarcely seen for a while. Others had to cancel family vacations to meet deadlines.\par \par \uc2 Scott Powell\uc2 , \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 's chief operating officer, said the bank appreciates the hard work employees are putting into this effort. The bank has support systems in place for employees in the control-evaluation process. In general, there is nothing unique about the timelines, he said.\par \par \uc2 "We're always talking about how we can make our programs better, and we regularly make enhancements," he said. "If we thought for a second we were creating a process that wasn't achievable, we would modify it."\par \par \uc2 Years before the fake-accounts scandal, \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  enjoyed a reputation as a well-run, low-risk place. Underneath, executives pressed an aggressive sales culture. Branch employees sometimes opened customer accounts using fictitious or unauthorized information to meet sales goals.\par \par \uc2 In September 2016, federal and local authorities slapped the bank with $185 million in fines for the fake accounts. Soon, problems were coming to light in nearly every corner of the bank.\par \par \uc2 Regulators concluded \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  couldn't manage its own risk. In 2018, the Fed took the unprecedented move of capping \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 's growth.\par \par \uc2 Scharf joined the following year, the third CEO since the scandal. He tossed out the bank's previous overhaul efforts and drew up a new plan to rework internal controls.\par \par \uc2 Scharf has said that when he took the job, he expected the bank to be further along than it was. In the four years since, things have taken longer than many expected.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230907ej970001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
