\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Next Big Disruption For Automakers Could Be Labor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Wilmot \par \uc2 719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Just when it is becoming easier to find a car, along comes another potential supply shock: a \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike.\par \par \uc2 Labor Day this year came at an unusually tense point in relations between U.S. vehicle assemblers and \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler owner \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The current four-year contract negotiated by the \uc2 UAW union\uc2  on behalf of factory employees at the Detroit Three expires on Sept. 14.\par \par \uc2 The two sides seem far from agreeing on a new contract. Ford on Thursday published the terms of its offer, including a 9% wage increase over the four years. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is asking for a 46% increase. Its plain-speaking president, Shaun Fain, responded in a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  address to members that Ford's wage proposal "insults our very worth."\par \par \uc2 And Ford is the most union-friendly of the Detroit Three. Although it trails \uc2 GM\uc2  in terms of U.S. market share, it assembles more vehicles locally. Its reputation for having a softer touch extends to Canada, where the \uc2 Unifor union\uc2  last week picked Ford as its potential target for pattern bargaining, ahead of the expiration of its own contract with the Detroit Three on Sept. 18.\par \par \uc2 While Fain didn't like Ford's offer, he accused \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  of "refusal to bargain in good faith" and said the \uc2 UAW\uc2  had filed a legal complaint against them. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  called the action frivolous, and \uc2 GM\uc2  said it was "an insult to the bargaining committees."\par \par \uc2 Even allowing for the theatrics of an all-but-public negotiation, none of this bodes well so close to the deadline. If no agreement comes, Fain has said the union will call a strike, potentially at all three companies. A recent vote authorized it to do so with 97% of members voting in favor.\par \par \uc2 With good reasons to dig in on both sides, it was always going to be a tough negotiation.\par \par \uc2 For workers, the recent increase in inflation eroded real pay even as higher vehicle prices padded the bottom lines of manufacturers. The existing contract is also riddled with concessions, such as a lower-pay employment tier, that date from Detroit's dog years at the start of the century. After a decade of healthy profits, the union wants to nix them.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, manufacturers are focused on the gap between their labor costs and those of nonunionized competitors, which any settlement will only widen. The total cost of the average \uc2 UAW\uc2 -organized assembly-line worker is $66 an hour, compared with $55 for nonunionized manufacturers such as \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , which now accounts for the majority of U.S. car sales, and just $45 for electric-vehicle specialist \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Additionally, Detroit profits may be on a downward trend. The analyst consensus is for \uc2 GM\uc2 's net profit to fall 4% this year and a further 9% in 2024, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 , as vehicle prices normalize and it sells more low-margin electric vehicles. A generous pay deal would add to the pain.\par \par \uc2 With so much at stake, a strike seems increasingly likely even if it would benefit nobody. An analysis by consultants at Anderson Economic Group put the cost to the U.S. economy of a 10-day strike affecting all three manufacturers at $5.6 billion. Four years ago the strike at \uc2 GM\uc2  dragged out for six weeks.\par \par \uc2 If a strike does happen, one consequence that Americans could feel far from Michigan is a continuation of the vehicle shortages that emptied dealer lots in 2021 and 2022. Inventories have improved this year, but they are still much lower today than they were when \uc2 GM\uc2  workers went on strike in 2019. The RAM and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  brands are exceptions, so a strike targeting \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  specifically -- a possibility given the company's fatter margins and heavier use of temporary workers -- might not have much market impact. But a broad strike certainly would.\par \par \uc2 Of course, tighter supply also could have the perverse effect of propping up vehicle margins at today's unusually high levels, at least for Detroit specialties such as pickup trucks and large sport-utility vehicles that face less nonunionized competition. When will the auto industry get back to normal after the pandemic? The answer always seems to be not yet.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich371\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5565
