\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Meet the Man Who Has Detroit on Edge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 1,499 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Shawn Fain glances at the wall of executive portraits at the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ' historic Black Lake conference center in Northern Michigan, there is one photo that always catches him off-guard.\par \par \uc2 His own.\par \par \uc2 "I look up and expect to see someone else," said Fain, who was elected this spring to lead the 400,000-member union. "It's very surreal."\par \par \uc2 Fain's ascent to the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's top office has taken the entire auto industry by surprise.\par \par \uc2 In the five months since he narrowly edged out the incumbent in a contentious runoff election, the 54-year-old \uc2 UAW\uc2  boss has tossed the typical union playbook, starting with turning his back on a traditional CEO-handshake ceremony that has for decades marked the start of labor talks in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 The new president and his executive board, many of whom ran as part of the same reform-minded caucus as Fain, are negotiating new labor contracts for about 146,000 hourly workers at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the global car company that now owns \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , Ram and Chrysler.\par \par \uc2 The existing contracts expire Sept. 14, and if a deal isn't reached by then, Fain has said he is ready to strike -- potentially not just one automaker but all three.\par \par \uc2 Elected in March, he became the first candidate outside the group's ruling caucus to unseat the incumbent in more than 70 years.\par \par \uc2 He has pledged to bring change to a union battered by a yearslong corruption scandal that has sent two past \uc2 UAW\uc2  presidents to prison. And he has insisted that workers should be sharing in the success of companies that have earned billions of dollars in profits each year. Members spent much of the Covid-19 pandemic working long days on assembly lines, Fain has argued, while corporate workers had the flexibility to clock in from home.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, he has been unusually outspoken about his frustrations with labor talks -- at one point, making a public show of throwing \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's bargaining proposals in the trash -- and has embraced fiery rhetoric that has riled up workers for a potential walkout and taken many auto executives aback.\par \par \uc2 Fain disclosed Thursday evening that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  had filed complaints with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  alleging that \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have been negotiating in bad faith, which the filings said amounted to an unfair labor practice. It is highly unusual for the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to make such a complaint at this stage of talks.\par \par \uc2 Both companies denied the allegations.\par \par \uc2 Under Fain, the union has brought forward a set of demands that includes winning back benefits that workers gave up in past talks to help the companies survive.\par \par \uc2 Fain, who started his union career as an electrician at a Chrysler plant in Kokomo, Ind., is also pushing for a shorter, 32-hour workweek and a 46% hourly wage increase over the four-year contract, which would be the largest in recent memory.\par \par \uc2 Some auto companies have called Fain's proposals excessive, and if fully implemented, damaging to their ability to compete with nonunion rivals like \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . One Wells Fargo analyst estimated the union's demands as they stand would add about $6 billion to $8 billion a year in labor expenses for each automaker.\par \par \uc2 In many ways, Fain is tapping a renewed vigor in the U.S. labor movement. Union negotiations for airline pilots and \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers have resulted in significant pay increases and better work-life balance, and the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's own strike at farm-equipment maker \uc2 Deere\uc2  in 2021 led to some sizable wins, including a 20% bump in wages.\par \par \uc2 His speeches are often peppered with quotes from Walter Reuther, the legendary 1940s union boss who was regarded as a reform-minded intellectual, and he counts civil-rights activist John Lewis and college-basketball coach John Wooden among his influences.\par \par \uc2 But when asked who his role model is, he hesitated for a moment.\par \par \uc2 "I am who I am," he replied. "I was raised to be my own person."\par \par \uc2 His office decor includes a placard that reads: "I don't sugar coat s -- . I'm not Willy Wonka."\par \par \uc2 Fain grew up in Kokomo, home to large manufacturing facilities now owned by \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He spent his younger years door-knocking and putting up yard signs with his politically active parents. His father was a local policeman and his mother was a nurse.\par \par \uc2 Fain's Sunday family dinners included relatives who worked at Chrysler and a nearby electronics plant owned by \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , giving him a real sense of their triumphs and struggles, he said.\par \par \uc2 He still carries in his pocket one of his grandfather's Chrysler pay stubs from 1940, a reminder of Fain's family ties with the union.\par \par \uc2 Early in Fain's career, he was laid off and went on a government aid program to pay for his child-rearing expenses.\par \par \uc2 "I'm glad I had that experience, because it really taught me what was important," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, Fain became more active in local politics, once running unsuccessfully for a spot on the county council in 2006.\par \par \uc2 He also rose within the union. He has been involved with the union's national negotiations in different capacities since 2009. During this time, he developed a reputation for at times clashing with \uc2 UAW\uc2  leadership.\par \par \uc2 "If I don't agree, I'm going to tell you I don't agree," Fain said. "I'm not going to do things just because you tell me to."\par \par \uc2 In late 2021, union members voted to change the way its leaders were elected in the wake of a federal civil settlement related to the corruption scandal.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, the union's president and other top officers would be elected directly by members, rather than by local chapter leaders.\par \par \uc2 Fain saw his moment. He won with a less than 1% margin, marking a turning point for the union and throwing a wild card at the automakers, who suddenly found themselves dealing with a mostly new \uc2 UAW\uc2  bargaining team.\par \par \uc2 Fain immediately hired staffers with successful records at other unions and regularly connects with members via weekly \uc2 Facebook\uc2  livestreams.\par \par \uc2 One of his first moves was to withhold the union's endorsement of President Biden's re-election bid, a major departure for a union that's consistently backed Democratic candidates. Fain has said that he wants to ensure first that Biden's agenda on electric vehicles will preserve and support union jobs.\par \par \uc2 Unlike past leaders, who tend to go quiet during negotiations, Fain has remained vocal, often throwing barbs at the companies and speaking candidly about their bargaining proposals.\par \par \uc2 In a symbolic show of change, he ditched the handshake ceremony with the car-company CEOs -- a tradition that dated back to at least the 1960s -- in favor of glad-handing with union members at different plants.\par \par \uc2 He said he won't shake the CEOs' hands until a contract is done.\par \par \uc2 "He's taking action," said Kimberly Speight, a factory worker for \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  in Michigan. "I like it. It's a direct approach."\par \par \uc2 Fain is also taking a rather unexpected strategy to bargaining this time around.\par \par \uc2 Historically, the union has picked one company to negotiate with first in hopes of reaching a deal that it can then use as a template for the other two, a process known as pattern bargaining.\par \par \uc2 If a deal can't be reached, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  targets that company for a strike.\par \par \uc2 This time, Fain and his team have opted to make all three car companies the so-called "target", believing that it will speed up the process and ultimately get a better deal for members by keeping the pressure on each one.\par \par \uc2 Many in the industry worry he is setting workers' expectations too high, which could make it difficult to get a deal ratified.\par \par \uc2 The White House, too, is on edge. Biden said earlier this month that he was concerned about the possibility of a strike, which would be economically disruptive.\par \par \uc2 Jim Cramer\uc2 , the host of CNBC's investing show "Mad Money," said the union's demands were so aggressive they'd significantly weaken the U.S. auto companies. "This man studied Trotsky," Cramer said during a broadcast this summer, referring to the Russian revolutionary.\par \par \uc2 When asked about Cramer's comments, Fain said he didn't know much about Trotsky and joked, sarcastically, how central Indiana is known as a "fertile ground for cornfields and communism."\par \par \uc2 Reflecting on his short tenure as union boss, Fain said the reality has yet to fully sink in.\par \par \uc2 "I don't want it to," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain\par \par \uc2 Picky eater: Orders plain baked potatoes.\par \par \uc2 Work breakaway: Played basketball in high school and has a hoop on his office door.\par \par \uc2 Aquatic hobbies: Water skiing and fishing.\par \par \uc2 Caffeine source: Hasn't had a drop of coffee in his life; prefers Coke.\par \par \uc2 Cinema snack: Sometimes stops by a movie theater just to grab popcorn.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230902ej920001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
