\par\par \b \uc2 Labor Unions Have Lost Their Way\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elisabeth Messenger \par \uc2 566 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 From the earliest days of American industry, labor unions played a major role in championing workers' rights, fighting for fair wages, safe working conditions and better benefits.\par \par \uc2 As Labor Day approaches, unions will be sure to remind us how much we have to thank them for. And they'll be right. But we'll also be reminded how far they've fallen. The labor movement successfully improved working conditions in the 20th century, but it has lost its way in the 21st -- veering from its original purpose and putting politics above workers' needs.\par \par \uc2 Many union members in America don't work in industry at all -- they are government employees. The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reports that in 2022 "the union membership rate of public-sector workers (33.1 percent) continued to be more than five times higher than the rate of private-sector workers." Government unions are some of the most powerful in the country -- especially the teachers unions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 , with more than 1.6 million members, paints a rosy picture of financial prosperity, with $152 million in assets and a net financial-position improvement of $15 million since 2020. A closer examination, however, reveals an alarming trend: the union increased its membership dues while losing more than 10,000 members since 2020.\par \par \uc2 No wonder teachers are turning away from the union when only 34% of its spending goes toward acting on behalf of its members. The \uc2 AFT\uc2  diverts large sums to political activities, supporting left-wing causes and candidates, taking no account of the diverse political affiliations among its members. Political spending accounted for 17.3% of the \uc2 AFT\uc2 's total outlays in the 2021-22 fiscal year. \uc2 AFT\uc2  President \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  pulls in roughly $488,000 a year -- more than eight times what a teacher makes.\par \par \uc2 The situation is arguably worse at the even larger \uc2 National Education Association\uc2 . It spent $49.2 million on political activities in 2021-22, surpassing the amount spent on membership representation by $3.5 million. Like the \uc2 AFT\uc2 , the NEA protected revenue from membership losses by hiking dues. The union's emphasis on financial investment further highlights its shift away from representing teachers and toward building its own wealth.\par \par \uc2 I work with public employees who feel abandoned by their unions. I've seen the positives and negatives of the labor movement. But it's clear that unions' emphasis on political activity is detrimental to members' interests and erodes the trust workers once placed in them.\par \par \uc2 Union alternatives -- such as Christian Educators and the Association of American Educators along with local, independent unions -- aren't part of a national monopoly and have the potential to fulfill the true purpose of unions: advocating workers' rights and improving working conditions.\par \par \uc2 The heart of the labor movement should be the pursuit of a better future for workers. We want unions to do better. National unions such as the \uc2 AFT\uc2  and the NEA should refocus their efforts on the needs and concerns of their members rather than pouring vast sums of money into divisive politics. True representation means standing up for all members, regardless of their political beliefs, and ensuring their voices are heard.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Messenger is CEO of Americans for Fair Treatment.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Weingarten: In Defense of the Teachers Union" -- WSJ Sept. 11, 2023)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020230902ej920001n\par }\page